OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Perfect-completeness-for-2-to-1-games-September-23-2026", "title": "Perfect completeness for 2-to-1 games", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Perfect-completeness-for-2-to-1-games-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "105", "family_title": "Perfect completeness for 2-to-1 games", "abstract_html": "We prove the 2-to-1 Games Conjecture with perfect completeness. For every fixed rational \\(\\delta\\in(0,1)\\), it is NP-hard to distinguish satisfiable 2-to-1 games from games of value at most <i>δ</i>, with a fixed alphabet and an explicitly listed unweighted multiset of constraints.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Perfect completeness for \\(2\\)-to-\\(1\\) games — paper Theorem 1.1.** A projection game is a finite bipartite multigraph with disjoint vertex sets \\(U,V\\), a nonempty multiset of edge occurrences, finite answer sets …", "statement": "<p>**Perfect completeness for \\(2\\)-to-\\(1\\) games — paper Theorem 1.1.**</p><p>A projection game is a finite bipartite multigraph with disjoint vertex sets \\(U,V\\), a nonempty multiset of edge occurrences, finite answer sets, and a projection map for each occurrence. A labeling satisfies an occurrence \\(e=(u,v)\\) when its right answer equals the image of its left answer under \\(\\pi_e\\). The value is the maximum fraction of satisfied occurrences. Parallel occurrences may have different maps.</p><p>An exact \\(2\\)-to-\\(1\\) game has common left alphabet \\([2q]\\), common right alphabet \\([q]\\), and\n\\[\n|\\pi_e^{-1}(b)|=2\\qquad(e\\in E,\\ b\\in[q]).\n\\]</p><p>For every fixed rational \\(\\delta\\in(0,1)\\), there are an integer \\(q=q(\\delta)\\ge2\\) and a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from \\(3\\)-SAT to exact \\(2\\)-to-\\(1\\) games such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. a satisfiable formula produces a game of value \\(1\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">2. an unsatisfiable formula produces a game of value at most \\(\\delta\\).</p><p>The output contains a nonempty explicit list of unweighted edge occurrences and an explicit table for every projection. The running time is polynomial in the ordinary binary input length; its degree and constants may depend on the fixed \\(\\delta\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix rational \\(\\delta\\in(0,1)\\) and set \\(\\varepsilon=\\delta/3\\).</p><p>Propositions 1–7 establish the source, construction, sampling comparisons, decoding, rank reduction, and repeated-source contradiction. Proposition 8 chooses their parameters in dependency order and produces a polynomially describable preliminary game with perfect completeness, at-most-two fibers, and NO value less than \\(\\varepsilon\\).</p><p>Proposition 9 completes its maps to exact \\(2\\)-to-\\(1\\) maps on fixed alphabets \\([2q]\\), \\([q]\\), preserving perfect completeness and raising NO value by at most \\(\\delta/3\\).</p><p>Proposition 10 explicitly enumerates the weighted game and replaces its rational weights by a nonempty explicit unweighted multiset at further value loss at most \\(\\delta/3\\). Hence NO value is at most\n\\[\n\\varepsilon+\\delta/3+\\delta/3=\\delta.\n\\]\nAll tables have exactly two preimages per right answer, and the deterministic running time is polynomial in binary input length for the fixed \\(\\delta\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Maximum \\(k\\)-colorable subgraph — paper Corollary 1.2.** There are absolute constants \\(C>0\\) and an integer \\(k_0\\ge3\\) such that, for every fixed integer \\(k\\ge k_0\\), it is NP-hard to distinguish, for a finite …", "statement": "<p>**Maximum \\(k\\)-colorable subgraph — paper Corollary 1.2.**</p><p>There are absolute constants \\(C&gt;0\\) and an integer \\(k_0\\ge3\\) such that, for every fixed integer \\(k\\ge k_0\\), it is NP-hard to distinguish, for a finite unweighted graph \\(G=(W,F)\\) with \\(F\\ne\\varnothing\\):</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(G\\) is \\(k\\)-colorable;</p><p class=\"li num\">2. every coloring \\(\\chi:W\\to[k]\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\frac{|\\{\\{u,v\\}\\in F:\\chi(u)\\ne\\chi(v)\\}|}{|F|}\n\\le 1-\\frac1k+C\\frac{\\log k}{k^2}.\n\\]</p><p>The fraction is the proportion of properly colored edges, equivalently the fraction cut by the partition into \\(k\\) color classes.</p>", "proof": "<p>Theorem 1 supplies the exact-two perfect-completeness premise of Guruswami and Sinop’s reduction [16, Conjecture 3.4 and Theorem 3.26].</p><p>Their regularization and reduction preserve perfect completeness, giving \\(k\\)-colorable YES graphs. Their Remark 2.6 transfers hardness to unweighted graphs. Their soundness\n\\[\n1-\\frac1k+O\\!\\left(\\frac{\\log k}{k^2}\\right)\n\\]\nis therefore unconditional.</p><p>Choose an absolute upper bound \\(C\\) for the lower-order term and enlarge \\(k_0\\) so the two cases are disjoint. All reduction parameters, including the soundness required from Theorem 1, are fixed once \\(k\\) is fixed.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Satisfiable Not-Two and three-query PCPs — paper Corollary 1.3.** Let \\(\\operatorname{NTW}(a,b,c)\\) be the Boolean predicate accepting exactly zero, one, or three true inputs.", "statement": "<p>**Satisfiable Not-Two and three-query PCPs — paper Corollary 1.3.**</p><p>Let \\(\\operatorname{NTW}(a,b,c)\\) be the Boolean predicate accepting exactly zero, one, or three true inputs.</p><p>For every fixed \\(0&lt;\\varepsilon&lt;3/8\\), it is NP-hard to distinguish a satisfiable instance of constraints\n\\[\n\\operatorname{NTW}(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3),\n\\]\nwhere each literal may be a variable or its negation, from an instance in which every assignment satisfies at most a \\(5/8+\\varepsilon\\) fraction of constraints.</p><p>Consequently, every language in NP has a polynomial-time verifier using \\(O(\\log n)\\) random bits and three nonadaptive proof-bit queries, with perfect completeness and soundness at most \\(5/8+\\varepsilon\\), where \\(n\\) is the input length.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply O’Donnell and Wu’s reduction [25, Theorem 2.1 and Corollary 2.2, author manuscript]. Its premise requires at-most-\\(d\\)-to-\\(1\\) projections for some fixed \\(d\\).</p><p>Take \\(d=2\\), use the uniform distribution on the explicit edge occurrences, and choose a fixed rational \\(\\delta\\in(0,1)\\) below their required soundness threshold. Theorem 1 supplies this premise with perfect completeness.</p><p>Their folded verifier permits all eight literal-sign variants of Not-Two and gives both the satisfiable-instance hardness and the three-nonadaptive-query PCP conclusion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "games and projection-game repetition — paper Section 2.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "A finite weighted projection game \\(G\\) has question sets \\(U,V\\), nonempty question-dependent legal answer sets \\(L_u,R_v\\), a nonempty finite set of edge occurrences, a probability distribution \\(\\mu\\) on occurrences …", "statement": "<p>A finite weighted projection game \\(G\\) has question sets \\(U,V\\), nonempty question-dependent legal answer sets \\(L_u,R_v\\), a nonempty finite set of edge occurrences, a probability distribution \\(\\mu\\) on occurrences, and total maps\n\\[\n\\pi_e:L_{u(e)}\\to R_{v(e)}.\n\\]\nIts value is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{val}(G)=\n\\max_{a,b}\\Pr_{e\\sim\\mu}\n[\\pi_e(a(u(e)))=b(v(e))].\n\\]\nEach player receives only its own question. Independent private randomness, or shared randomness independent of the sampled edge, cannot increase value.</p><p>For \\(k\\ge1\\), \\(G^{\\otimes k}\\) samples \\(k\\) independent occurrences and accepts only if all coordinates accept. Repeated questions retain separate answer coordinates. Set \\(\\operatorname{val}(G^{\\otimes0})=1\\).</p><p>The following hold.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. For every such \\(G\\), \\(0&lt;g&lt;1\\), and \\(k\\ge1\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{val}(G)\\le1-g\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\operatorname{val}(G^{\\otimes k})\n\\le(1-g^2/16)^k.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. There is a fixed \\(\\gamma_0&gt;0\\) and a deterministic polynomial-time transformation of a \\(3\\)-CNF formula \\(\\varphi\\) into a nonempty \\(3\\)-CNF formula \\(\\Psi\\), each clause using three distinct variables, such that satisfiability is preserved and, on unsatisfiable inputs, every assignment violates at least a \\(\\gamma_0\\) fraction of clause occurrences.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For every required fixed constant \\(\\sigma&gt;0\\), a fixed power \\(t\\ge1\\) gives a polynomial-size source game\n\\[\nG_{\\mathrm{src}}=G_\\Psi^{\\otimes t}\n\\]\nwith perfect completeness and, on unsatisfiable inputs,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{val}(G_{\\mathrm{src}})\\le\\sigma.\n\\]\nHere \\(G_\\Psi\\) samples a uniform clause occurrence and a uniform position. Its left answer is one of the seven satisfying assignments of that clause, its right answer is a bit for the selected variable, and its map selects that bit.</p><p>A source left question is an ordered tuple of \\(t\\) clause occurrences, with answer domain of size \\(7^t\\); a source right question is an ordered tuple of variable IDs, with answer domain \\(\\mathbb F_2^t\\). Repeated IDs do not identify answer coordinates. Every source projection is surjective. At each elementary clause domain, every single coordinate and every pair of distinct coordinates are surjective.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 1.1 gives \\(\\Psi\\). For each clause occurrence \\(C\\), let\n\\[\nA_C=\\{x\\in\\mathbb F_2^3:x\\text{ satisfies }C\\}.\n\\]\nIts seven elements record actual variable values; clause signs enter only in the definition of \\(A_C\\).</p><p>A satisfying assignment gives value \\(1\\) in \\(G_\\Psi\\). Conversely, a right labeling defines an assignment to all variables. At every clause violated by that assignment, every legal left answer differs in at least one of the three positions. Therefore\n\\[\n\\varphi\\text{ unsatisfiable}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\operatorname{val}(G_\\Psi)\\le1-\\gamma_0/3.\n\\]\nChoose fixed \\(t\\) with\n\\[\n(1-\\gamma_0^2/144)^t\\le\\sigma.\n\\]\nLemma 1.2 gives the asserted source soundness, while product completeness remains one.</p><p>A clause excludes just one of the eight triples. Every prescribed pair of coordinate values has two extensions, at least one of which remains legal. Thus each coordinate and each pair of distinct coordinates is surjective. Products give surjectivity of every source projection.</p><p>Since \\(t\\) is fixed, explicit enumeration of the source power is polynomial. Finally, fixing complete randomized answer tables and averaging proves that private or edge-independent shared randomness cannot increase game value.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Perfect-completeness PCP gap — paper Theorem 2.1.** There are a constant \\(\\gamma_0>0\\) and a deterministic polynomial-time map taking any Boolean \\(3\\)-CNF formula \\(\\varphi\\) to a nonempty \\(3\\)-CNF formula \\(\\Psi\\) …", "statement": "<p>**Perfect-completeness PCP gap — paper Theorem 2.1.**</p><p>There are a constant \\(\\gamma_0&gt;0\\) and a deterministic polynomial-time map taking any Boolean \\(3\\)-CNF formula \\(\\varphi\\) to a nonempty \\(3\\)-CNF formula \\(\\Psi\\) whose clauses each use three distinct variables, such that:</p><p class=\"li\">if \\(\\varphi\\) is satisfiable, then \\(\\Psi\\) is satisfiable;</p><p class=\"li\">if \\(\\varphi\\) is unsatisfiable, every assignment violates at least a \\(\\gamma_0\\) fraction of clauses of \\(\\Psi\\).</p><p>Clause occurrences are explicitly listed, and the binary encoding length of \\(\\Psi\\) is polynomial in that of \\(\\varphi\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Dinur’s perfect-completeness PCP formulation [7, Definition 8.1 and Theorem 8.1, February 13, 2007 author manuscript]. It produces an explicitly listed family of \\(N\\) bounded-arity predicates over a fixed alphabet, satisfiable in the YES case, with a fixed rejected fraction \\(\\theta&gt;0\\) under every assignment in the NO case.</p><p>Encode each alphabet symbol injectively by a fixed number of bits. For each predicate require valid symbol encodings and acceptance of the decoded symbols. Decode an invalid block to a fixed default symbol. Whenever an original predicate rejects the decoded assignment, its Boolean version rejects: either a queried block is invalid, or all blocks are valid and rejection is unchanged. Thus every Boolean assignment violates at least \\(\\theta N\\) Boolean predicates. After identifying repeated queried variables, each involves at most a fixed number \\(k_0\\) of distinct bits.</p><p>For each rejecting assignment, form the clause excluding precisely that assignment. The conjunction of at most \\(2^{k_0}\\) such clauses is exactly the Boolean predicate. A rejecting zero-bit predicate contributes the empty clause.</p><p>For a clause \\(\\ell_1\\vee\\cdots\\vee\\ell_k\\), \\(k\\ge4\\), use fresh variables \\(y_1,\\ldots,y_{k-3}\\) and replace it by\n\\[\n(\\ell_1\\vee\\ell_2\\vee y_1),\n\\]\n\\[\n(\\neg y_j\\vee\\ell_{j+2}\\vee y_{j+1})\n\\quad(1\\le j\\le k-4),\n\\]\n\\[\n(\\neg y_{k-3}\\vee\\ell_{k-1}\\vee\\ell_k).\n\\]\nIf all \\(\\ell_h\\) are false, the first clause forces \\(y_1=1\\), the middle clauses propagate this value, and the last fails. If the original clause is true, set \\(y_j=1\\) exactly when \\(\\ell_1,\\ldots,\\ell_{j+1}\\) are all false. This satisfies every replacement clause. Use disjoint auxiliary variables for every replacement.</p><p>There is a fixed bound \\(B\\ge1\\) on the number of clauses per predicate. Hence the resulting formula has at most \\(BN\\) clauses, while every NO assignment violates at least \\(\\theta N\\), giving gap \\(\\widehat\\gamma=\\theta/B\\).</p><p>Remove tautological clauses and simplify repeated literals. Replace a remaining clause \\(C\\) on \\(k\\le3\\) distinct variables by all \\(2^{3-k}\\) clauses obtained by adjoining every sign pattern on \\(3-k\\) fresh variables. If \\(C\\) is true, every extension is true. If \\(C\\) is false, exactly one replacement clause is false for every assignment to the fresh variables. For an empty clause use all eight sign patterns on three fresh variables.</p><p>Every violated original clause therefore still contributes a violation, while the number of clauses increases by at most eight. Take \\(\\gamma_0=\\widehat\\gamma/8\\). If normalization leaves no clauses, use a fixed satisfiable clause on three fresh variables; this cannot occur on a NO output. An input with no clauses can likewise be handled directly. All steps preserve perfect completeness and are deterministic and polynomial.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Weighted projection-game repetition — paper Theorem 2.2.** For every finite weighted projection game \\(G\\) with the conventions of Proposition 1, every \\(0<g<1\\), and every integer \\(k\\ge1\\) …", "statement": "<p>**Weighted projection-game repetition — paper Theorem 2.2.**</p><p>For every finite weighted projection game \\(G\\) with the conventions of Proposition 1, every \\(0&lt;g&lt;1\\), and every integer \\(k\\ge1\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{val}(G)\\le1-g\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\operatorname{val}(G^{\\otimes k})\n\\le(1-g^2/16)^k.\n\\]\nThe bound is uniform in the question sets, answer sets, and probability distribution on edge occurrences.</p>", "proof": "<p>This is the parallel repetition theorem of Dinur and Steurer [11, Corollary 1.2]. Their Sections 2.1–2.2 allow nonnegative weights, parallel edges, and independent products of questions. Interchanging players matches the projection direction here.</p><p>Embed question-dependent legal answers in one finite common alphabet and give illegal answers no accepting pairs. Such partial projection constraints are allowed in the cited formulation. Legal defaults show that this extension does not change value, so the theorem applies directly.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Inverse matrix structure and random row advice — paper Section 2.2–2.3.** All spaces in this proposition are finite-dimensional over \\(\\mathbb F_2\\).", "statement": "<p>**Inverse matrix structure and random row advice — paper Section 2.2–2.3.**</p><p>All spaces in this proposition are finite-dimensional over \\(\\mathbb F_2\\). For \\(K=\\mathbb F_2^\\ell\\) and a space \\(H\\), identify\n\\[\nK\\otimes H=\\operatorname{Hom}(H^*,K).\n\\]\nA matrix \\(M\\) is a list of \\(\\ell\\) rows in \\(H\\). For \\(q\\in H^*\\), \\(Mq\\in K\\) is its column value.</p><p>A row and column slice is a nonempty affine set\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=\n\\{M:AM=U,\\ Mq_b=t_b\\ (1\\le b\\le c)\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(A:K\\to\\mathbb F_2^a\\). Counts refer to specified row combinations and column values; independence is not required. A slice with no column specifications is a row fiber.</p><p>The stationary rank-one step is\n\\[\nM\\longmapsto M+ah,\n\\]\nwhere \\(M\\), \\(a\\in K\\setminus\\{0\\}\\), and \\(h\\in H\\) are independent and uniform.</p><p>A partial table \\(f:K\\otimes H\\to Y\\cup\\{\\perp\\}\\) has a finite defined range \\(Y\\). Defined equality means equality in \\(Y\\); two undefined values never count.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. For every \\(0&lt;\\eta&lt;1\\), there are \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\), \\(r\\ge1\\), and \\(\\ell_0\\), depending only on \\(\\eta\\), such that, for every \\(\\ell\\ge\\ell_0\\), every \\(H,Y\\), and every partial table satisfying\n\\[\n\\Pr[f(M)=f(M+ah)\\ne\\perp]\\ge\\eta,\n\\]\nsome \\(y\\in Y\\) has density at least \\(\\alpha\\) on a nonempty slice with at most \\(r\\) row combinations and \\(r\\) column values. The constants are independent of \\(\\dim H\\) and \\(|Y|\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Fix \\(0&lt;\\eta&lt;1\\), and take \\(\\alpha,r,\\ell_0\\) from the preceding assertion with threshold \\(\\eta/2\\). If defined equality is at least \\(\\eta\\), call the row fiber of \\(A:K\\to\\mathbb F_2^r\\) good when some nonempty slice in it, specified by at most \\(r\\) columns, has one defined value of density at least \\(\\alpha\\). Then\n\\[\n\\Pr_M[M\\text{ belongs to a good row fiber for some }A]\\ge\\eta/4\n\\]\nand, for independent uniform \\(A,M\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr_{A,M}[\\text{the fiber of }AM\\text{ for }A\\text{ is good}]\n\\ge(\\eta/4)2^{-r\\ell}.\n\\]\nWitnesses can be chosen deterministically from \\(f,A,AM\\). Any chosen nonempty slice specified by at most \\(r\\) columns has conditional probability at least \\(2^{-r\\ell}\\) in its row fiber.</p><p>Both assertions apply unchanged to quotient spaces \\(H/B\\). Their column functionals pull back to elements of \\(\\operatorname{Ann}(B)\\subseteq H^*\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The inverse assertion is Lemma 2.4.</p><p>For the row-advice assertion, let \\(T\\) be the union of all good row fibers over the \\(2^{r\\ell}\\) possible maps \\(A:K\\to\\mathbb F_2^r\\). Suppose its uniform measure were less than \\(\\eta/4\\). Erase the table on \\(T\\), obtaining \\(f_0\\). Stationarity gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Pr[f_0(M)=f_0(M+ah)\\ne\\perp]\n&amp;\\ge\\Pr[f(M)=f(M+ah)\\ne\\perp]\\\\\n&amp;\\quad-\\Pr[M\\in T]-\\Pr[M+ah\\in T]\n&gt;\\eta/2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nLemma 2.4 supplies a defined value of \\(f_0\\) of density at least \\(\\alpha\\) on a nonempty slice with at most \\(r\\) row and column specifications. Pad its row map with zero coordinates to codomain \\(\\mathbb F_2^r\\). Since \\(f_0\\) is a restriction of \\(f\\), this witnesses a good row fiber for \\(f\\), entirely contained in \\(T\\), where \\(f_0\\) is undefined. This contradiction proves\n\\[\n\\Pr[M\\in T]\\ge\\eta/4.\n\\]</p><p>For each \\(M\\in T\\), at least one of the \\(2^{r\\ell}\\) maps has a good fiber through \\(M\\). Averaging gives\n\\[\n\\Pr_{A,M}[\\text{good fiber}]\\ge(\\eta/4)2^{-r\\ell}.\n\\]\nNo assertion for each particular row map is needed.</p><p>Fix finite orders and select the first witnessing columns, values, and defined response from \\(f,A,AM\\). Within a nonempty row fiber, at most \\(r\\) columns impose at most \\(r\\ell\\) scalar equations. A consistent affine system therefore has conditional measure at least \\(2^{-r\\ell}\\).</p><p>Apply the same argument directly to \\(H/B\\), adding and eliminating dummy columns in that quotient. Its column functionals belong to \\((H/B)^*\\), so their pullbacks annihilate \\(B\\). This includes the zero-dimensional quotient.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Grassmann expansion consequence — paper Theorem 2.3, external input.** The binary Grassmann graph on an \\(N\\)-dimensional space has its \\(\\ell\\)-dimensional subspaces as vertices; distinct vertices are adjacent when …", "statement": "<p>**Grassmann expansion consequence — paper Theorem 2.3, external input.**</p><p>The binary Grassmann graph on an \\(N\\)-dimensional space has its \\(\\ell\\)-dimensional subspaces as vertices; distinct vertices are adjacent when their intersection has dimension \\(\\ell-1\\). The retention of a nonempty vertex set is the probability that a uniform vertex of that set has a uniformly chosen neighbor in the set.</p><p>For each \\(0&lt;\\zeta&lt;1\\), there are \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\), \\(r\\ge1\\), and \\(\\ell_0\\) such that, for every \\(\\ell\\ge\\ell_0\\) and then every sufficiently large \\(N\\), every nonempty vertex set of retention at least \\(\\zeta\\) has density at least \\(\\alpha\\) in an interval\n\\[\n\\{L:A_0\\subseteq L\\subseteq B_0,\\ \\dim L=\\ell\\},\n\\qquad\n\\dim A_0+\\operatorname{codim}B_0\\le r.\n\\]\nThe constants depend only on \\(\\zeta\\); the threshold for \\(N\\) may also depend on \\(\\ell\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For sets of density at most \\(1/2\\), apply Khot–Minzer–Safra [20, Theorem 1.12, ECCC TR18-006, revision 2], writing expansion as one minus retention; their Definitions 1.8 and 1.10 give the expansion and interval conventions.</p><p>For a set of density greater than \\(1/2\\), use the whole interval \\(A_0=\\{0\\}\\), \\(B_0=\\mathbb F_2^N\\). Replace the cited density constant by its minimum with \\(1/2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Matrix set extraction in large dimension — paper Lemma 2.4.** For every \\(0<\\eta<1\\), there are \\(\\alpha>0\\), \\(r\\ge1\\), and \\(\\ell_0\\), depending only on \\(\\eta\\), such that, for each \\(\\ell\\ge\\ell_0\\) and all …", "statement": "<p>**Matrix set extraction in large dimension — paper Lemma 2.4.**</p><p>For every \\(0&lt;\\eta&lt;1\\), there are \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\), \\(r\\ge1\\), and \\(\\ell_0\\), depending only on \\(\\eta\\), such that, for each \\(\\ell\\ge\\ell_0\\) and all sufficiently large \\(m\\), the following holds.</p><p>If nonempty \\(S\\subseteq\\operatorname{Mat}_{\\ell,m}(\\mathbb F_2)\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\Pr_{M\\text{ uniform in }S,\\ a\\ne0,\\ h}\n[M+ah^{\\mathsf t}\\in S]\\ge\\eta,\n\\]\nwhere \\(a\\in\\mathbb F_2^\\ell\\setminus\\{0\\}\\) and \\(h\\in\\mathbb F_2^m\\) are independent uniform vectors, then \\(S\\) has density at least \\(\\alpha\\) in a nonempty row and column slice with at most \\(r\\) equations of each kind.</p>", "proof": "<p>Increase the threshold on \\(m\\) so that \\(2^{-m}\\le\\eta/2\\). Conditional on \\(h\\ne0\\), retention is at least\n\\[\n\\frac{\\eta-2^{-m}}{1-2^{-m}}\\ge\\eta/2.\n\\]</p><p>Associate to \\(M\\) its graph\n\\[\nL_M=\\{(x,M^{\\mathsf t}x):x\\in\\mathbb F_2^\\ell\\}\n\\subseteq\\mathbb F_2^\\ell\\oplus\\mathbb F_2^m.\n\\]\nThese form the chart \\(\\mathcal C\\) where projection onto the first summand is invertible. Since\n\\[\n\\dim(L_M\\cap L_{M'})=\\ell-\\operatorname{rank}(M-M'),\n\\]\nchart neighbors are precisely nonzero rank-one changes. Over \\(\\mathbb F_2\\), each nonzero rank-one matrix has exactly one factorization \\(ah^{\\mathsf t}\\) with both factors nonzero. The chart degree is\n\\[\n(2^\\ell-1)(2^m-1).\n\\]\nThe full Grassmann degree is\n\\[\n(2^\\ell-1)(2^{m+1}-2),\n\\]\nso exactly half the neighbors of each chart vertex remain in the chart.</p><p>Consequently \\(\\widetilde S=\\{L_M:M\\in S\\}\\) has full Grassmann retention at least \\(\\eta/4\\). Lemma 2.1 gives an interval \\([A_0,B_0]\\) of bounded co-order on which \\(\\widetilde S\\) has density at least \\(\\alpha\\). Its intersection with \\(\\mathcal C\\) is nonempty, and restricting the denominator to that intersection cannot decrease density.</p><p>A basis \\((d_b,s_b)\\) of \\(A_0\\) gives the row equations\n\\[\nd_b^{\\mathsf t}M=s_b^{\\mathsf t}.\n\\]\nA basis \\((t_b,q_b)\\) of \\(B_0^\\perp\\) gives the column equations\n\\[\nMq_b=t_b.\n\\]\nThus the intersection is a slice with at most \\(r\\) equations of each kind. Fixing \\(\\ell\\) and then taking \\(m\\) sufficiently large meets the ambient-dimension threshold.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Eliminating dummy columns — paper Lemma 2.5.** Let \\(H,D,K\\) be finite binary vector spaces.", "statement": "<p>**Eliminating dummy columns — paper Lemma 2.5.**</p><p>Let \\(H,D,K\\) be finite binary vector spaces. Write a matrix on \\(H\\oplus D\\) as\n\\[\n(X,Y)\\in\\operatorname{Hom}(H^*,K)\\oplus\\operatorname{Hom}(D^*,K).\n\\]\nSuppose the affine slice\n\\[\n\\widetilde{\\mathcal S}=\n\\{(X,Y):AX=U_H,\\ AY=U_D,\\ Xq_b+Yd_b=t_b\\ (1\\le b\\le c)\\}\n\\]\nis nonempty, with \\(A:K\\to\\mathbb F_2^a\\), \\(q_b\\in H^*\\), and \\(d_b\\in D^*\\).</p><p>Its image under \\((X,Y)\\mapsto X\\) is a nonempty row and column slice with the same \\(a\\) row combinations and at most \\(c\\) column values. Every image point has equally many lifts. Uniform measure on the padded slice therefore projects to uniform measure on its image.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(W=\\ker A\\), and define\n\\[\nq(\\lambda)=\\sum_b\\lambda_bq_b,\\qquad\nd(\\lambda)=\\sum_b\\lambda_bd_b,\\qquad\nt(\\lambda)=\\sum_b\\lambda_bt_b\n\\]\nfor \\(\\lambda\\in\\mathbb F_2^c\\).</p><p>The image consists exactly of matrices satisfying\n\\[\nAX=U_H,\\qquad\nXq(\\lambda)=t(\\lambda)\\quad(\\lambda\\in\\ker d).\n\\]\nA basis of \\(\\ker d\\) gives at most \\(c\\) column equations. Necessity follows by combining equations whose dummy arguments vanish.</p><p>For sufficiency, fix \\((X_*,Y_*)\\in\\widetilde{\\mathcal S}\\). For an \\(X\\) satisfying the projected equations, define on \\(\\operatorname{im}d\\)\n\\[\nL_0(d(\\lambda))=(X_*-X)q(\\lambda).\n\\]\nThis is well-defined by the equations for \\(\\lambda\\in\\ker d\\), and its values lie in \\(W\\). Extend it to \\(L:D^*\\to W\\), and set \\(Y=Y_*+L\\). Then \\(AY=U_D\\), and\n\\[\nXq(\\lambda)+Yd(\\lambda)\n=Xq(\\lambda)+Y_*d(\\lambda)+(X_*-X)q(\\lambda)\n=t(\\lambda).\n\\]\nThus \\(Y\\) is a lift.</p><p>Every lift arises from such an extension, and the number of extensions is\n\\[\n2^{\\dim W(\\dim D-\\operatorname{rank}d)},\n\\]\nindependent of \\(X\\). Zero dimensions and rank-deficient \\(A\\) are allowed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Inverse theorem for a finite-range partial table — paper Theorem 2.6.** Use the matrix and partial-table conventions of Proposition 2.", "statement": "<p>**Inverse theorem for a finite-range partial table — paper Theorem 2.6.**</p><p>Use the matrix and partial-table conventions of Proposition 2. For every \\(0&lt;\\eta&lt;1\\), there are \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\), \\(r\\ge1\\), and \\(\\ell_0\\), depending only on \\(\\eta\\), such that, for every \\(\\ell\\ge\\ell_0\\), every finite binary space \\(H\\), and every finite range \\(Y\\), the condition\n\\[\n\\Pr_{M,a,h}[f(M)=f(M+ah)\\ne\\perp]\\ge\\eta\n\\]\nimplies that some defined value has density at least \\(\\alpha\\) on a nonempty slice with at most \\(r\\) row combinations and \\(r\\) column values.</p><p>The constants are independent of \\(\\dim H\\) and \\(|Y|\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Lemma 2.2 with threshold \\(\\eta\\). Add a dummy space \\(D\\) until \\(H\\oplus D\\) meets its column-dimension threshold, and define\n\\[\n\\widetilde f(X,Y)=f(X).\n\\]\nUniform matrices and uniform step factors project to the corresponding uniform variables on \\(H\\), so defined-equality probability is unchanged, including when \\(H=0\\).</p><p>For each nonempty defined fiber \\(S_y=\\widetilde f^{-1}(y)\\), let \\(\\mu_y\\) be its uniform ambient measure and \\(\\theta_y\\) its retention. Partitioning by the first value gives\n\\[\n\\Pr[\\widetilde f(\\widetilde M)\n=\\widetilde f(\\widetilde M+a\\widetilde h)\\ne\\perp]\n=\\sum_{y\\in Y}\\mu_y\\theta_y.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\sum_y\\mu_y\\le1\\), some \\(\\theta_y\\ge\\eta\\). There is no factor depending on the number of fibers.</p><p>Lemma 2.2 gives a padded slice where this fixed value has density at least \\(\\alpha\\). Project it using Lemma 2.3. Uniform measure projects uniformly, and \\(\\widetilde f\\) depends only on the real matrix, so the same density holds on the projected slice without increasing either constraint count.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Tree function spaces, canonical questions, one-bit maps, and joint folding — paper Section 3.1–3.4.** Fix the source power \\(t\\) of Proposition 1.", "statement": "<p>**Tree function spaces, canonical questions, one-bit maps, and joint folding — paper Section 3.1–3.4.**</p><p>Fix the source power \\(t\\) of Proposition 1. A clause occurrence \\(c\\) has three distinct variable IDs \\(v(c,1),v(c,2),v(c,3)\\) and domain\n\\[\n\\Omega_c=A_c=\\{x\\in\\mathbb F_2^3:x\\text{ satisfies }c\\}.\n\\]</p><p>Fix positive integers \\(d,n_1,\\ldots,n_d\\), row counts \\(\\ell_1,\\ldots,\\ell_d\\), and product counts \\(R_1,\\ldots,R_d\\). Let \\(\\mathcal T\\) be the rooted ordered tree with root at level \\(d\\), leaves at level \\(0\\), and \\(n_p\\) children per level-\\(p\\) node. Write \\(\\mathcal T_p\\) for its level-\\(p\\) nodes and \\(K_p=\\mathbb F_2^{\\ell_p}\\).</p><p>An original tuple \\(E\\) places a source left question at every leaf. Elementary slots are formal pairs \\((C,k)\\), \\(C\\in\\mathcal T_0\\), \\(k\\in[t]\\). All slots are separate product factors, even when question IDs repeat.</p><p>A mixed tuple \\(Q\\) may replace selected leaf questions by right questions along source projections. At a projected elementary slot the domain is \\(\\mathbb F_2\\), tagged by the selected variable ID. Let \\(\\Omega_D(Q)\\) be the product below node \\(D\\), and \\(\\Omega(Q)\\) the root product. Projection from \\(E\\) to a mixed tuple \\(O\\) gives surjections\n\\[\n\\pi_D:\\Omega_D(E)\\to\\Omega_D(O).\n\\]</p><p>Define actual pointwise function spaces, as in paper Definition 3.1:\n\\[\nH_C(Q)=\\mathbb F_2^{\\Omega_C(Q)}\n\\quad(C\\text{ a leaf}),\n\\]\n\\[\nH_C(Q)^2=\\operatorname{span}\\{gh:g,h\\in H_C(Q)\\},\n\\qquad\nH_D(Q)=\\sum_{C\\text{ child of }D}H_C(Q)^2.\n\\]\nDescendant functions are extended by ignoring other arguments.</p><p>At each nonleaf \\(D\\) of level \\(p\\), an array is\n\\[\nM_D\\in K_p\\otimes H_D(Q).\n\\]\nFor the array family \\(\\mathbf M\\), define\n\\[\n\\Psi_{Q,\\mathbf M}(x)=(M_D(x))_{D\\text{ nonleaf}}\n\\in\\prod_{p=1}^d\\prod_{D\\in\\mathcal T_p}K_p.\n\\]\nFor nonzero \\(a\\in K_p\\), let \\(Q_{D,a}\\) quotient the \\(D\\)-block by \\(\\langle a\\rangle\\), fixing all other blocks, and put\n\\[\n\\Psi^{D,a}_{Q,\\mathbf M}=Q_{D,a}\\Psi_{Q,\\mathbf M}.\n\\]</p><p>For any finite-valued function \\(f:\\Omega(Q)\\to Y\\):</p><p class=\"li\">discard a slot if \\(f\\) ignores it;</p><p class=\"li\">reduce a used clause slot to position \\(j\\) if \\(f\\) globally factors through that selected bit;</p><p class=\"li\">retain all other used clause slots in full;</p><p class=\"li\">retain used variable slots as bits.</p><p>The reduced domain is \\(\\Omega_f\\), its reduction map is \\(r_f\\), and \\(f=\\widetilde f\\,r_f\\). A retained clause coordinate records formal location, clause occurrence ID, and triple domain. A retained bit records formal location and variable ID; a reduced clause’s occurrence and selected position are discarded.</p><p>The canonical key is its side tag, retained typed coordinates, and unordered partition\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_f=\\{\\widetilde f^{-1}(y):y\\in\\operatorname{im}f\\}.\n\\]\nA legal answer is a part \\(P\\in\\mathcal P_f\\). Its restored output is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{out}_f(P)=y\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nP=\\widetilde f^{-1}(y).\n\\]\nKeys contain no output names, array coordinates, projection positions, or additional sampling choices.</p><p>For \\(f=\\Psi_{E,\\mathbf M}\\), \\(g=Q_{D,a}f\\), the edge map is\n\\[\n\\pi_{f,D,a}(P)=\n\\operatorname{out}_g^{-1}\n\\bigl(Q_{D,a}\\operatorname{out}_f(P)\\bigr).\n\\]</p><p>These constructions have the following properties:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. every edge map is a surjection of nonempty legal sets with fibers of size at most two;</p><p class=\"li num\">2. a satisfying source assignment gives one labeling satisfying every such edge;</p><p class=\"li num\">3. pullback from projected domains preserves canonical keys and restored outputs exactly;</p><p class=\"li num\">4. the joint folding and product-generator identities of Lemmas 3.6–3.7 below hold for every legal labeling;</p><p class=\"li num\">5. for fixed parameters all local legal answer-set sizes are bounded independently of the source instance.</p><p>Vertices may include all obtainable mixed-domain keys. A labeling on positive-weight vertices is extended arbitrarily, but legally, to unused keys.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 3.1–3.3 justify the function spaces, support reduction, and exact sharing. Lemma 3.4 proves the at-most-two maps, and Lemma 3.5 proves perfect completeness. Lemmas 3.6–3.7 give the exact identities forced by joint legality.</p><p>If \\(N=|\\mathcal T_0|\\), every mixed domain has size at most \\(7^{tN}\\); therefore every legal partition has at most \\(7^{tN}\\) parts. For fixed parameters this is a uniform finite bound. Truth-table enumeration computes support reductions, partitions, restorations, and edge maps.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Pointwise-space inclusions — paper Lemma 3.2.** In the setting of Proposition 3, all spaces contain \\(1\\), and \\( H_C(Q)\\subseteq H_C(Q)^2. \\) For every proper descendant \\(C\\) of \\(D\\) …", "statement": "<p>**Pointwise-space inclusions — paper Lemma 3.2.**</p><p>In the setting of Proposition 3, all spaces contain \\(1\\), and\n\\[\nH_C(Q)\\subseteq H_C(Q)^2.\n\\]\nFor every proper descendant \\(C\\) of \\(D\\),\n\\[\nH_C(Q)^2\\subseteq H_D(Q).\n\\]\nPullback by \\(\\pi_D\\) injectively identifies \\(H_D(O)\\) with a subspace of \\(H_D(E)\\), preserving pointwise multiplication.</p>", "proof": "<p>Every elementary domain is nonempty, so extension by ignoring arguments is injective. Leaves contain \\(1\\); inductively a child’s \\(1=1\\cdot1\\) supplies the parent’s constant. Multiplication by \\(1\\) gives \\(H_C\\subseteq H_C^2\\).</p><p>For a child, \\(H_C^2\\subseteq H_D\\) is the definition. For a more distant descendant, iterate\n\\[\nH_C^2\\subseteq H_{D'}\\subseteq H_{D'}^2\n\\]\nalong the route to \\(D\\).</p><p>Every elementary selected-bit projection is surjective, hence so is their product. Pullback is therefore injective on full function spaces and preserves sums and products. Induction through the sum-of-products definition proves the asserted inclusions. The summands need not form a direct sum; their constants coincide.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Simultaneous support reduction — paper Lemma 3.3.** Let \\(f:\\Omega(Q)\\to Y\\) be as in Proposition 3.", "statement": "<p>**Simultaneous support reduction — paper Lemma 3.3.**</p><p>Let \\(f:\\Omega(Q)\\to Y\\) be as in Proposition 3. A clause slot is reducible to position \\(j\\) when, for all fixed other arguments \\(x_{-s}\\) and all \\(u,u'\\in\\Omega_c\\) with \\(u_j=u'_j\\),\n\\[\nf(x_{-s},u)=f(x_{-s},u').\n\\]</p><p>If a used clause slot is reducible, its position is unique. All support reductions can be performed simultaneously, giving a surjection \\(r_f:\\Omega(Q)\\to\\Omega_f\\) and a unique \\(\\widetilde f\\) with \\(f=\\widetilde f\\,r_f\\).</p><p>The reduced domain and its fiber partition depend only on\n\\[\nx\\sim_f x'\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad f(x)=f(x'),\n\\]\nnot on output names.</p>", "proof": "<p>If a clause slot factors through distinct positions \\(j,k\\), fix the other arguments and write its section as both \\(g(u_j)\\) and \\(h(u_k)\\). Pair-surjectivity gives\n\\[\ng(b)=h(b')\\qquad((b,b')\\in\\mathbb F_2^2).\n\\]\nBoth functions are constant. Every section is therefore constant, so the slot is unused.</p><p>Each coordinate reduction is a surjection: the constant map for an unused slot, the selected coordinate for a reducible slot, or the identity otherwise. Their product is surjective. If two points have the same reduced image, replace their coordinates one at a time. Each replacement preserves \\(f\\), proving that \\(f\\) is constant on reduction fibers and hence induces a unique \\(\\widetilde f\\).</p><p>Unusedness and reducibility are predicates about equality of \\(f\\)-values. Thus they, their unique reductions, and the induced partition are determined by \\(\\sim_f\\).</p><p>The reducibility position must be global. For example,\n\\[\nf(x,b)=(1+b)x_1+bx_2\n\\]\non \\(\\Omega_c\\times\\mathbb F_2\\) uses one position in each fixed-\\(b\\) section but has no single global position. Its clause slot is retained.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Sharing across projected domains — paper Lemma 3.4.** Let \\(O\\) arise from \\(E\\) by source projections, let \\( \\pi:\\Omega(E)\\to\\Omega(O) \\) be their product, and let \\(f:\\Omega(O)\\to Y\\).", "statement": "<p>**Sharing across projected domains — paper Lemma 3.4.**</p><p>Let \\(O\\) arise from \\(E\\) by source projections, let\n\\[\n\\pi:\\Omega(E)\\to\\Omega(O)\n\\]\nbe their product, and let \\(f:\\Omega(O)\\to Y\\). For either side tag, \\(f\\) and \\(f\\pi\\) have literally identical canonical keys, legal parts, and restored outputs.</p>", "proof": "<p>At an unprojected slot, unusedness and every candidate singleton factorization agree for \\(f\\) and \\(f\\pi\\), because projection of all other arguments is surjective.</p><p>At a projected slot, unusedness is likewise equivalent. If used, \\(f\\pi\\) factors through the selected position, which is unique by Lemma 3.2. Both representations retain exactly the same formal location, selected variable ID, and bit domain; neither retains the clause occurrence or projection position.</p><p>Their reduced domains thus coincide. Their induced functions are identical by surjectivity, so their partitions and restored values agree.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**One-bit maps — paper Lemma 3.5.** Every map \\(\\pi_{f,D,a}\\) in Proposition 3 is a well-defined surjection between nonempty legal answer sets and has at most two preimages per right label.", "statement": "<p>**One-bit maps — paper Lemma 3.5.**</p><p>Every map \\(\\pi_{f,D,a}\\) in Proposition 3 is a well-defined surjection between nonempty legal answer sets and has at most two preimages per right label.</p><p>For a fixed labeling, acceptance is exactly equality of restored outputs through \\(Q_{D,a}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Restoration bijects legal parts with attained values. Since \\(g=Q_{D,a}f\\), every attained \\(g\\)-value has an attained \\(f\\)-preimage, proving well-definedness and surjectivity.</p><p>The kernel of \\(Q_{D,a}\\) consists of zero and the vector supported at \\(D\\) with value \\(a\\). Every fiber on the whole output space has exactly two elements, and its intersection with \\(\\operatorname{im}f\\) has at most two. Restoration transfers this bound to legal parts and gives the acceptance description. Different reduced input supports at the endpoints do not affect the argument.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Perfect completeness — paper Proposition 3.6.** If the source clause instance has a satisfying assignment, one common legal labeling satisfies every constraint of Proposition 3, for every choice of its positive …", "statement": "<p>**Perfect completeness — paper Proposition 3.6.**</p><p>If the source clause instance has a satisfying assignment, one common legal labeling satisfies every constraint of Proposition 3, for every choice of its positive integer parameters.</p><p>Consequently, every game supported on these constraints has value one.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\xi\\) satisfy all source clauses. At a canonical key, assign each retained full clause coordinate its satisfying triple under \\(\\xi\\), and each retained bit coordinate its variable value under \\(\\xi\\). These choices give a point of the reduced domain even when IDs recur. Label the vertex by the part containing that point.</p><p>The prescription depends only on the typed coordinates and partition in the key, so it is consistent at shared vertices. On an original tuple \\(E\\), \\(\\xi\\) gives a legal point \\(x_\\xi\\in\\Omega(E)\\). Its support reduction is the prescribed point at both endpoints. Restored outputs are therefore \\(f(x_\\xi)\\) and \\(g(x_\\xi)\\), which agree through \\(Q_{D,a}\\). Lemma 3.4 proves acceptance of every edge.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Joint folding — paper Lemma 3.7.** Fix a full array question on \\(\\Omega(Q)\\), and a nonleaf \\(D\\).", "statement": "<p>**Joint folding — paper Lemma 3.7.**</p><p>Fix a full array question on \\(\\Omega(Q)\\), and a nonleaf \\(D\\). For each row \\(h\\) of \\(M_D\\), choose\n\\[\nb_h=c_h1+\n\\sum_{C,u,v}\\lambda_{h,C,u,v}(M_C)_u(M_C)_v,\n\\qquad c_h,\\lambda_{h,C,u,v}\\in\\mathbb F_2,\n\\]\nwhere \\(C\\) ranges over any collection of proper nonleaf descendants of \\(D\\).</p><p>Replace \\((M_D)_h\\) by \\((M_D)_h+b_h\\), fixing all other arrays, to obtain \\(\\widetilde{\\mathbf M}\\). This is valid and has exactly the same canonical key. For a fixed legal label at that key, restored outputs satisfy\n\\[\n\\widetilde y_C=y_C\\quad(C\\ne D),\n\\]\n\\[\n(\\widetilde y_D)_h\n=(y_D)_h+c_h+\n\\sum_{C,u,v}\\lambda_{h,C,u,v}(y_C)_u(y_C)_v.\n\\]</p><p>The same assertion holds for quotient questions whenever the shift uses only unchanged scalar rows actually displayed there. A shift at a quotient block is interpreted through its quotient map.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 3.1 puts every shift term in \\(H_D(Q)\\). On the full output product, fix all blocks other than \\(D\\) and add the displayed polynomial in descendant blocks to block \\(D\\). Applying this transformation twice is the identity: its input blocks do not change, and the characteristic is two.</p><p>Thus the new joint function is a bijective transformation of the old one, so both have the same equality relation. Lemma 3.2 gives the same support reduction and unordered partition. A fixed part represents the same reduced arguments in both questions, and evaluating on any such argument gives the stated formulas.</p><p>For quotient blocks use the identical triangular transformation on the displayed quotient spaces. Its arguments remain unchanged, so its inverse is again itself. No free action on labels or representative-assignment choice is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.7", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Joint evaluation of product generators — paper Lemma 3.8.** Fix a legal response to a full array question, a node \\(D\\), and a collection \\(\\mathcal C\\) of proper nonleaf descendants.", "statement": "<p>**Joint evaluation of product generators — paper Lemma 3.8.**</p><p>Fix a legal response to a full array question, a node \\(D\\), and a collection \\(\\mathcal C\\) of proper nonleaf descendants. Put\n\\[\nB=\\operatorname{span}\n\\left(\n\\{1\\}\\cup\n\\{(M_C)_u(M_C)_v:C\\in\\mathcal C,\\ 1\\le u,v\\le\\ell_{\\operatorname{level}(C)}\\}\n\\right)\n\\subseteq H_D(Q).\n\\]\nIf \\(y_C\\) are the restored outputs, there is a unique \\(b_y\\in B^*\\) such that\n\\[\nb_y(1)=1,\\qquad\nb_y((M_C)_u(M_C)_v)=(y_C)_u(y_C)_v.\n\\]\nIt extends to \\(H_D(Q)^*\\). Every extension \\(z\\) satisfies \\(z(1)=1\\) and gives, at each \\(C\\), the tested matrix \\(y_Cy_C^{\\mathsf t}\\), of rank at most one.</p>", "proof": "<p>One assignment \\(x\\in\\Omega(Q)\\) realizes the whole legal joint response. Point evaluation at \\(x\\) restricts to a functional with the stated generator values. It respects all pointwise relations, including those involving different descendants.</p><p>The generators span \\(B\\), so this functional is unique. Extend a basis of \\(B\\) to a basis of \\(H_D(Q)\\) and choose arbitrary values on added basis vectors. Every extension retains the displayed generator values, hence the outer-product matrices.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Recursive sampling, cut comparisons, sparse projection, posterior bounds, and bias — paper Sections 3.5 and 4.** Use the source, tree, and spaces of Proposition 3.", "statement": "<p>**Recursive sampling, cut comparisons, sparse projection, posterior bounds, and bias — paper Sections 3.5 and 4.**</p><p>Use the source, tree, and spaces of Proposition 3. Sample independent source left questions at all leaves to obtain \\(E\\). Independently sample a uniform root-to-leaf path\n\\[\nP=(D_d,D_{d-1},\\ldots,D_0).\n\\]</p><p>Conditional on \\(E,P\\), define \\(\\operatorname{Sample}(D_0)\\) to be a uniform element of \\(H_{D_0}(E)\\). At a path node \\(D_p\\), with path child \\(C_*=D_{p-1}\\), independently draw\n\\[\nU_C\\sim\\operatorname{Unif}(H_C(E)^2)\\quad(C\\ne C_*),\n\\]\n\\[\nG_h,H_h\\sim\\operatorname{Sample}(C_*)\\quad(1\\le h\\le R_p)\n\\]\nand return\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Sample}(D_p)\n=\\sum_{C\\ne C_*}U_C+\\sum_{h=1}^{R_p}G_hH_h.\n\\]\nWrite \\(\\nu_{D_p}\\) for this scalar law. Every invocation uses a fresh entire recursive tape.</p><p>For each nonleaf path node, independently generate buckets\n\\[\nh_{p,v}\\sim\\nu_{D_p}\\quad(0\\ne v\\in K_p)\n\\]\nand set\n\\[\nM_{D_p}=\\sum_{v\\ne0}v\\otimes h_{p,v}.\n\\]\nOff-path arrays are independent uniforms in their spaces. All actual arrays and all scalar call trees have separate tapes.</p><p>Independently draw \\(a_p\\) uniformly from \\(K_p\\setminus\\{0\\}\\). The preliminary game chooses \\(p\\) uniformly from \\([d]\\) and uses the constraint of Proposition 3 at \\(D_p,a_p\\). Only canonical keys are questions; the path and tapes are not appended.</p><p>Let\n\\[\n\\operatorname{TV}(\\mu,\\nu)=\\tfrac12\\sum_x|\\mu(x)-\\nu(x)|.\n\\]</p><p>For any fixed finite family of calls at a cut \\(D_p\\), there is a finite child likelihood bound \\(L_p\\), independent of \\(n_p\\), higher branching, question identities, and instance size, with the following properties.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. After forgetting the path below \\(D_p\\), the original retained law differs from the unrestricted law—independent uniform cut calls and uniform descendant arrays—by at most\n\\[\n\\epsilon_p^{\\mathrm{cut}}=\\frac{L_p}{2\\sqrt{n_p}}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. At a level-\\(i\\) cut, independently mark children with probability\n\\[\n\\beta_i=n_i^{-2/3},\n\\]\nwhere \\(n_i&gt;1\\) is a cube. Project every leaf in each marked child to its source right question, giving \\(O\\). Stop ancestor calls at \\(D_i\\), replace them by independent uniforms in \\(H_{D_i}(O)\\), and sample \\(M_{D_i}\\) and descendant arrays independently uniformly in the projected spaces. Reconstruct ancestors from the stopped expressions. Denote this modified law by \\(\\mathbb P_i\\).</p><p>After lifting functions to \\(E\\) and forgetting \\(O\\), projections, and marks, its distance from the unrestricted \\(i\\)-law is at most\n\\[\n\\epsilon_i^{\\mathrm{spr}}\n=\\tfrac12\\sqrt{(1+\\beta_i^2L_i^2)^{n_i}-1}.\n\\]</p><p>For a common observation permissible at an upper cut \\(j&gt;i\\), its distance from the unrestricted \\(j\\)-law is at most\n\\[\n\\epsilon_i^{\\mathrm{spr}}\n+\\epsilon_i^{\\mathrm{cut}}\n+\\epsilon_j^{\\mathrm{cut}}.\n\\]\nSuch observations may retain upper stopped values and all displayed descendant arrays, but not the hidden continuation of the path or internal decompositions of upper stopped values.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Conditional on lifted raw child blocks and additional independent choices, but not projection metadata, each child has posterior marking probability at most\n\\[\nq_i=\\frac{\\beta_iL_i}{1-\\beta_i}.\n\\]\nAny adaptively selected set of at most \\(m\\) children contains a marked child with probability at most \\(mq_i\\). In particular, for two bilinear forms \\(F_0,F_1\\) on \\(H_{D_i}(E)\\) determined by these lifted data,\n\\[\n\\Pr\\!\\left[\nF_0\\ne F_1,\\ \nF_0|_{H_{D_i}(O)\\times H_{D_i}(O)}\n=F_1|_{H_{D_i}(O)\\times H_{D_i}(O)}\n\\right]\\le2q_i.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">4. There is an absolute \\(R_{\\min}\\) such that, if all \\(R_p\\ge R_{\\min}\\), all scalar laws are quarter-balanced: each nonzero functional takes either bit with probability at least \\(1/4\\). At an original path node, or above a modified cut,\n\\[\n\\left|\\mathbb E_{h\\sim\\nu_{D_p}}(-1)^{f(h)}\\right|\n\\le(7/8)^{R_p}\n\\quad(0\\ne f).\n\\]\nUniform cut and leaf laws have zero nontrivial bias.</p><p>If \\(A:K_j\\to\\mathbb F_2^r\\), \\(W=\\ker A\\), and \\(j&gt;i\\), the hidden sum\n\\[\nX=\\sum_{0\\ne v\\in W}v\\otimes h_{j,v}\n\\in W\\otimes H_{D_j}(O)\n\\]\nhas every nonzero character biased by at most \\((7/8)^{R_j}\\), even after revealing all other actual matrices and buckets with \\(Av\\ne0\\), conditional on domains and path through the modified cut. This law is determined by \\(O\\), that path, \\(A\\), and the fixed parameters.</p><p>All comparisons allow fixed finite enlargements of call families. Once row counts, product counts, source alphabet, and lower branching are fixed, increasing \\(n_p\\) makes the cut error, sparse-projection error, and posterior discrepancy arbitrarily small.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 4.1–4.5 establish the stated comparisons, posterior bounds, and bias.</p><p>The game law is finite and supported on the constraints of Proposition 3, so it has perfect completeness.</p><p>At a cut \\(p\\), the finite block bound uses only source alphabet, row and product counts, and branching below \\(p\\). After fixing those quantities,\n\\[\n\\frac{L_p}{\\sqrt{n_p}}\\to0,\\qquad\n\\sqrt{(1+n_p^{-4/3}L_p^2)^{n_p}-1}\\to0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{2n_p^{-2/3}L_p}{1-n_p^{-2/3}}\\to0.\n\\]\nFor the middle limit use\n\\[\n(1+n_p^{-4/3}L_p^2)^{n_p}\n\\le\\exp(L_p^2n_p^{-1/3}).\n\\]\nHigher branching changes only exterior records and deterministic reconstruction maps, so already obtained bounds persist.</p><p>The scalar induction requires only the common quarter-balance minimum below an upper level. A requested upper tolerance \\(\\lambda_j\\) is met by choosing \\(R_j\\) with \\((7/8)^{R_j}\\le\\lambda_j\\), without imposing a finer lower-level accuracy.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Stopped-call representation — paper Lemma 4.1.** Fix \\(E\\) and the path through \\(D_p\\), but not its child.", "statement": "<p>**Stopped-call representation — paper Lemma 4.1.**</p><p>Fix \\(E\\) and the path through \\(D_p\\), but not its child. Put\n\\[\nb_k=2^{\\ell_k}-1,\\qquad c_h=2R_h.\n\\]\nThere is an exterior record \\(\\Xi_p\\), generated outside \\(D_p\\)’s subtree before sampling the path continuation, such that ancestor arrays are deterministic expressions in \\(\\Xi_p\\) and pending scalar calls at \\(D_p\\).</p><p>The pending ancestor calls and own buckets number at most\n\\[\nT_p=\\sum_{k=p}^d b_k\\prod_{h=p+1}^kc_h.\n\\]\nIf \\(e_k\\) additional scalar draws are requested at each \\(k\\ge p\\), a sufficient count is\n\\[\nT_p(e)=\\sum_{k=p}^d(b_k+e_k)\\prod_{h=p+1}^kc_h.\n\\]\nThe count is independent of \\(n_p\\) and higher branching.</p><p>Conditional on the full path, distinct stopped calls have independent tapes. Pending ancestor calls, own buckets, and actual descendant arrays use distinct tapes. Already assembled ancestor arrays are deterministic outputs, not independent exterior information.</p><p>After uniformizing the cut, its actual matrix can instead use \\(\\ell_p\\) independent uniform rows. The count becomes\n\\[\n\\widehat T_p=\n\\ell_p+\\sum_{k=p+1}^d b_k\\prod_{h=p+1}^kc_h.\n\\]\nAdding one independent row for a rank-one step gives \\(\\widehat T_p+1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Expand every scalar bucket originating at a path node \\(D_k\\), \\(k\\ge p\\). At level \\(h&gt;p\\), a call produces \\(R_h\\) products of two fresh calls at its path child, hence \\(c_h\\) recursive calls. Stop each branch when it first reaches \\(D_p\\). One bucket at level \\(k\\) produces exactly\n\\[\n\\prod_{h=p+1}^kc_h\n\\]\nstopped calls. Summing gives the two stated bounds.</p><p>Ordinary-child contributions encountered above the cut are supported outside its subtree. They can be generated before choosing the continuation below \\(D_p\\). Assign each invocation its own tape indexed by origin, bucket, and sequence of factor indices. These indices remain distinct even when calls visit the same geometric node.</p><p>Filling the stopped leaves in the finite sum-and-product expressions reconstructs all ancestors. Higher branching can enlarge the exterior expressions but not the number of stopped leaves.</p><p>Independent uniform buckets map surjectively onto a uniform cut matrix; alternatively use independent uniform rows, giving \\(\\widehat T_p\\). Predetermine all calls before any row map chooses visible buckets. Selecting or discarding calls afterward changes no count.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**One hidden child — paper Lemma 4.2.** Use the original sampler and a fixed level-\\(p\\) cut \\(D=D_p\\).", "statement": "<p>**One hidden child — paper Lemma 4.2.**</p><p>Use the original sampler and a fixed level-\\(p\\) cut \\(D=D_p\\). Fix \\(E\\), the path through \\(D\\), and a permissible exterior record. Retain at most \\(t_{\\mathrm{cut}}\\) scalar draws at the cut and all displayed arrays below it, but forget the path continuation and internal factor tapes.</p><p>For a child \\(C\\), put \\(V_C=H_C(E)^2\\) and\n\\[\n\\mathcal X_C=\nV_C^{t_{\\mathrm{cut}}}\n\\times\n\\prod_{\\substack{T\\preceq C\\\\T\\text{ nonleaf}}}\n(K_{\\operatorname{level}(T)}\\otimes H_T(E)).\n\\]\nA block records each child’s separate contribution to every cut draw and all its actual subtree arrays.</p><p>If source answer domains have size at most \\(a\\), put \\(N_0=1\\) and \\(N_k=\\prod_{h=1}^kn_h\\). A valid dimension bound is\n\\[\n\\dim\\mathcal X_C\n\\le\nD_p(t_{\\mathrm{cut}})\n=\na^{N_{p-1}}\n\\left(\nt_{\\mathrm{cut}}\n+\\sum_{k=1}^{p-1}\\ell_k\\prod_{h=k+1}^{p-1}n_h\n\\right).\n\\]\nTake \\(L_p=2^{D_p(t_{\\mathrm{cut}})}\\), or a larger common bound.</p><p>The retained original law \\(\\mathcal A_p\\) and unrestricted law \\(\\mathcal U_p\\) satisfy\n\\[\n\\operatorname{TV}(\\mathcal A_p,\\mathcal U_p)\n\\le\\frac{L_p}{2\\sqrt{n_p}}.\n\\]\nThis applies to deterministic reconstructions and independent auxiliary choices, including candidate tests at levels at least \\(p\\), but not a tag identifying the next path child.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(P_C\\) be uniform on \\(\\mathcal X_C\\). Independent uniform child contributions sum to independent uniform cut draws: the linear addition map\n\\[\n\\prod_CV_C\\to H_D(E),\\qquad(u_C)_C\\mapsto\\sum_Cu_C\n\\]\nis surjective, so all fibers have the same size. This does not require direct summands.</p><p>Let \\(J\\) be the next path child, uniform among \\(n=n_p\\) children. Conditional on \\(J=C\\), every other child block has law \\(P_{C'}\\), independently. Average the special block’s law over its path continuation to obtain \\(Q_C\\). Since every \\(P_C\\)-atom has mass \\(2^{-\\dim\\mathcal X_C}\\), its density \\(q_C=dQ_C/dP_C\\) is at most \\(L_p\\).</p><p>Relative to \\(P=\\prod_CP_C\\), the original block density is\n\\[\nZ=\\frac1n\\sum_Cq_C(X_C).\n\\]\nThe summands are independent under \\(P\\) and have expectation one. Hence\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_P(Z-1)^2\n=\\frac1{n^2}\\sum_C\\mathbb E_{P_C}(q_C-1)^2\n\\le L_p^2/n.\n\\]\nCauchy–Schwarz gives the TV bound. Adding child contributions and reconstructing ancestor expressions is a common deterministic map, so the bound persists.</p><p>Extra calls only enlarge the finite blocks. Revealing \\(J\\) would replace the average density by \\(q_J\\) and lose the \\(n^{-1/2}\\) factor.</p><p>In the unrestricted law, the cut matrix\n\\[\nM=\\sum_{v\\ne0}v\\otimes h_v\n\\]\nis uniform because the bucket-to-matrix map is surjective. Replacing \\(h_a\\) by an independent uniform \\(h_a^*\\) gives\n\\[\nM^*=M+a\\otimes h,\\qquad h=h_a+h_a^*.\n\\]\nConditional on all old buckets, \\(h\\) is uniform and independent. Thus this is exactly the ordinary uniform rank-one step, independently of pending ancestor calls and descendant matrices.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Sparse projection comparison — paper Lemma 4.3.** At a level-\\(i\\) cut, use the modified law \\(\\mathbb P_i\\) of Proposition 4.", "statement": "<p>**Sparse projection comparison — paper Lemma 4.3.**</p><p>At a level-\\(i\\) cut, use the modified law \\(\\mathbb P_i\\) of Proposition 4. Fix \\(E\\), the path through \\(D_i\\), and an exterior record generated without projections. Lift all projected functions to \\(E\\), forgetting projection marks, choices, and \\(O\\).</p><p>For a child likelihood bound \\(L_i\\) on all retained raw blocks,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{TV}(\\mathcal M_i,\\mathcal U_i)\n\\le\\epsilon_i^{\\mathrm{spr}}\n=\\frac12\\sqrt{(1+\\beta_i^2L_i^2)^{n_i}-1}.\n\\]\nAll raw child summands and descendant arrays may be retained.</p><p>The modified-to-original observation distance is at most\n\\[\n\\epsilon_i^{\\mathrm{spr}}+\\epsilon_i^{\\mathrm{cut}}.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(j&gt;i\\), consider an observation that is a common deterministic function of:</p><p class=\"li\">the upper exterior record \\(\\Xi_j\\);</p><p class=\"li\">retained scalar values at \\(D_j\\), including its own buckets and any extra cut draws;</p><p class=\"li\">all displayed arrays strictly below \\(D_j\\);</p><p class=\"li\">independent auxiliary choices.</p><p>It must not use the hidden path below \\(D_j\\) or internal decompositions of those upper scalar values. Its distance from the unrestricted-\\(j\\) observation is at most\n\\[\n\\epsilon_i^{\\mathrm{spr}}\n+\\epsilon_i^{\\mathrm{cut}}\n+\\epsilon_j^{\\mathrm{cut}}.\n\\]\nIn particular, a selected lower test is permitted only when determined without that hidden path.</p>", "proof": "<p>For each child \\(C\\), let \\(P_C\\) be the uniform unprojected block law. Conditional on marking \\(C\\), average projected uniform functions over its source projections and lift them to obtain \\(Q_C\\), with density \\(q_C\\le L_i\\).</p><p>Different children use independent marks, projections, and tapes. Their product law is\n\\[\n\\prod_C((1-\\beta_i)P_C+\\beta_iQ_C),\n\\]\nwith density \\(\\prod_C(1+\\beta_i(q_C-1))\\) relative to \\(\\prod_CP_C\\). Independence gives\n\\[\n1+\\chi^2\\!\\left(\n\\prod_C((1-\\beta_i)P_C+\\beta_iQ_C)\n\\,\\middle\\|\\,\\prod_CP_C\n\\right)\n=\n\\prod_C(1+\\beta_i^2\\chi^2(Q_C\\|P_C))\n\\le(1+\\beta_i^2L_i^2)^{n_i}.\n\\]\nApply \\(\\operatorname{TV}\\le\\tfrac12\\sqrt{\\chi^2}\\).</p><p>Uniform cut rows arise by the same addition map in both laws. Bucket representations can be used when retaining a bucket replacement. Extra calls add finite coordinates. All reconstructed ancestors and candidate quotients are functions of these blocks, the exterior record, and independent directions; data processing applies without conditioning on their realized values.</p><p>The comparison with the original law is the triangle through \\(\\mathcal U_i\\) and Lemma 4.2. For the upper comparison, average the \\(i\\)-cut comparison over the path prefix between \\(j\\) and \\(i\\), then apply Lemma 4.2 at \\(j\\). A pending value at \\(j\\) is an output of the \\(i\\)-cut reconstruction and a retained value in the \\(j\\)-cut comparison. Its internal subtree decomposition cannot be retained there.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Posterior bound for adaptive witnesses — paper Lemma 4.4.** In the modified law \\(\\mathbb P_i\\), condition on \\(E\\), the path through \\(D_i\\), and a permissible exterior record.", "statement": "<p>**Posterior bound for adaptive witnesses — paper Lemma 4.4.**</p><p>In the modified law \\(\\mathbb P_i\\), condition on \\(E\\), the path through \\(D_i\\), and a permissible exterior record. Let \\(X=(X_C)_C\\) contain all lifted raw child blocks, including a fixed number of extra uniform cut rows.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal G\\) be generated by these blocks and additional independent choices, such as directions or fixed complete strategy tapes, but not projected-question metadata or marks.</p><p>For every child, almost surely,\n\\[\n\\Pr(C\\text{ marked}\\mid\\mathcal G)\n\\le q_i=\\frac{\\beta_iL_i}{1-\\beta_i}.\n\\]\nIf at most \\(m\\) children are selected \\(\\mathcal G\\)-measurably, their chance of containing a marked child is at most \\(mq_i\\). The assertions persist under coarsening.</p><p>For bilinear forms \\(F_0,F_1\\) on \\(H_{D_i}(E)\\) determined by these lifted data,\n\\[\n\\Pr[\nF_0\\ne F_1,\\ \nF_0|_{H_{D_i}(O)\\times H_{D_i}(O)}\n=F_1|_{H_{D_i}(O)\\times H_{D_i}(O)}\n]\\le2q_i.\n\\]\nNo success, right response, slice, or witness-match event is conditioned upon.</p>", "proof": "<p>Bayes’s formula and child independence give\n\\[\n\\Pr(C\\text{ marked}\\mid X)\n=\n\\frac{\\beta_iq_C(X_C)}\n{1-\\beta_i+\\beta_iq_C(X_C)}\n\\le\\frac{\\beta_iL_i}{1-\\beta_i}.\n\\]\nIndependent extra choices leave this formula unchanged. For an adaptively selected set \\(\\mathcal C(X)\\), condition first on \\(\\mathcal G\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\!\\left[\n\\sum_{C\\in\\mathcal C(X)}1_{\\{C\\text{ marked}\\}}\n\\,\\middle|\\,\\mathcal G\n\\right]\\le|\\mathcal C(X)|q_i\\le mq_i.\n\\]\nAverage and use a union bound. The tower property proves coarsened versions.</p><p>For the bilinear assertion put \\(\\Delta=F_0-F_1\\). If nonzero, use\n\\[\nH_{D_i}(E)=\\sum_CH_C(E)^2\n\\]\nto find children \\(C_1,C_2\\) and \\(x\\in H_{C_1}(E)^2\\), \\(y\\in H_{C_2}(E)^2\\) with \\(\\Delta(x,y)=1\\). Choose the first witness in fixed orders from the lifted data. The children may coincide.</p><p>If neither child is marked, both spaces remain unchanged and lie in \\(H_{D_i}(O)\\); equal restrictions are then impossible. The bad event is contained in marking one of at most two adaptively selected children. Apply the preceding bound.</p><p>This is an unconditional bad-event estimate, which may later be subtracted from success probability. It does not assert a posterior bound after conditioning on success.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Scalar bias and independent hidden buckets — paper Lemma 4.5.** There is an absolute integer \\(R_{\\min}\\) such that, when all \\(R_p\\ge R_{\\min}\\), the scalar and hidden-bucket bias assertions in Proposition 4 hold.", "statement": "<p>**Scalar bias and independent hidden buckets — paper Lemma 4.5.**</p><p>There is an absolute integer \\(R_{\\min}\\) such that, when all \\(R_p\\ge R_{\\min}\\), the scalar and hidden-bucket bias assertions in Proposition 4 hold.</p><p>They hold conditional on original domains and full path, or on \\(E,O\\), projection choices, and the path through a modified cut. The hidden-sum assertion remains valid on revealing original questions, other actual matrices, nonhidden buckets, and independent sampling tapes, but is not asserted after conditioning on a column slice, decoding success, or cleanliness.</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose \\(g,h\\) are independent draws from quarter-balanced laws on a binary space \\(V\\), and \\(B:V\\times V\\to\\mathbb F_2\\) is nonzero bilinear. The map \\(g\\mapsto B(g,\\cdot)\\) has proper kernel. A nonzero functional vanishing on that kernel shows\n\\[\n\\Pr[B(g,\\cdot)\\ne0]\\ge1/4.\n\\]\nFor every such \\(g\\), quarter balance of \\(h\\) gives either bit of \\(B(g,h)\\) probability at least \\(1/4\\). Thus each bit has unconditional probability at least \\(1/16\\), and\n\\[\n|\\mathbb E(-1)^{B(g,h)}|\\le7/8.\n\\]</p><p>Induct upward from uniform leaves, or from the modified uniform cut. For a nonzero functional \\(f\\) on a path-node space, if its restriction to any ordinary child’s square space is nonzero, that uniform summand makes \\(f(h)\\) exactly uniform.</p><p>Otherwise \\(f\\) vanishes on all ordinary child square spaces. These and the path child square space span the parent space, so \\(f\\) is nonzero on the latter. Since products span that square space,\n\\[\nB(g,h)=f(gh)\n\\]\nis a nonzero bilinear form on the child row space. Induction makes its independent factors quarter-balanced. The \\(R_p\\) independent products yield bias at most \\((7/8)^{R_p}\\). Choose \\(R_{\\min}\\) with \\((7/8)^{R_{\\min}}\\le1/2\\), so the resulting scalar law is again quarter-balanced.</p><p>For a nonzero character \\(\\Phi\\) of \\(W\\otimes H_{D_j}(O)\\), some nonzero \\(v\\in W\\) makes \\(h\\mapsto\\Phi(v\\otimes h)\\) nonzero. Fresh tapes give\n\\[\n\\mathbb E(-1)^{\\Phi(X)}\n=\n\\prod_{0\\ne v\\in W}\n\\mathbb E(-1)^{\\Phi(v\\otimes h_v)}.\n\\]\nOne factor has absolute value at most \\((7/8)^{R_j}\\); all others are at most one. If \\(W=0\\), there are no nonzero characters.</p><p>The hidden buckets remain independent of the specified other data because calls at the same node are never reused. Their intrinsic projected-space sampler uses only \\(O\\), the path to the cut, and fixed parameters, not \\(E\\), projection maps, or a missing lower-matrix component.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Upper decoding with a lower rank test — paper Proposition 5.4.** Use the preliminary game and modified laws of Proposition 4, and fix a legal labeling extended to all unused keys as in Proposition 3.", "statement": "<p>**Upper decoding with a lower rank test — paper Proposition 5.4.**</p><p>Use the preliminary game and modified laws of Proposition 4, and fix a legal labeling extended to all unused keys as in Proposition 3. For each candidate level \\(p\\), let \\(\\operatorname{Acc}_p\\) denote acceptance.</p><p>Suppose the game acceptance is at least \\(0&lt;\\varepsilon&lt;1\\), and \\(d\\varepsilon\\ge4\\). Assume the cut errors are small enough to give the transfers specified in Lemmas 5.1–5.4 below.</p><p>There are levels \\(i&lt;j\\) such that, in the modified-\\(i\\) law \\(\\mathbb P_i\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr_i(\\operatorname{Acc}_i\\cap\\operatorname{Acc}_j)\\ge c,\n\\qquad c=\\varepsilon^2/2.\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\nH=H_{D_j}(E),\\quad H'=H_{D_j}(O),\\quad\nH_i=H_{D_i}(E),\\quad H_i'=H_{D_i}(O),\n\\]\n\\[\nK=K_j=\\mathbb F_2^\\ell,\\quad \\ell=\\ell_j,\\quad\nM=M_{D_j},\\quad S=M_{D_i}.\n\\]</p><p>The construction in Lemmas 5.2–5.4 fixes constants \\(\\alpha,r,\\ell_0\\) from \\(c\\), and\n\\[\n\\kappa=c/2,\\quad\n\\eta=c^2/32,\\quad\nh_0=2^{-r\\ell},\\quad\ng=(\\eta^2/4)2^{-r\\ell},\\quad q_0=gh_0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\rho=\\kappa\\alpha/4,\\quad\nb=\\kappa\\alpha q_0/4,\\quad\n\\tau=\\rho h_0/4.\n\\]\nAssume\n\\[\n\\ell\\ge\\ell_0,\\qquad\n2^{-(\\ell-r)}&lt;\\rho/8,\n\\]\n\\[\n(7/8)^{R_j}\\le\\lambda\n\\le\\min\\{h_0/2,\\tau^2/2\\},\n\\]\nand every product count meets the absolute balance minimum of Proposition 4.</p><p>Choose a uniform independent row map \\(A:K\\to\\mathbb F_2^r\\), let \\(W=\\ker A\\), and split upper buckets as\n\\[\nM=M_{\\mathrm{known}}+X,\n\\]\n\\[\nM_{\\mathrm{known}}=\\sum_{v\\in K\\setminus W}v\\otimes h_v,\n\\qquad\nX=\\sum_{0\\ne v\\in W}v\\otimes h_v\\in W\\otimes H'.\n\\]</p><p>The left input is exactly\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\sigma\\!\\left(\nE,\\ \\text{path through }D_i,\\ A,\\ (M_D)_{D\\ne D_j},\n\\ (h_v)_{v\\in K\\setminus W}\n\\right),\n\\]\nwith all functions on \\(E\\)-domains. It contains full \\(S\\), but not \\(a_i,O\\), or projection metadata.</p><p>The right input is exactly\n\\[\n\\mathcal R=\n\\sigma\\!\\left(\nO,\\ \\text{path through }D_i,\\ A,\\ a_i,\\\n(M_D)_{D\\ne D_j,D_i},\\\nS\\bmod\\langle a_i\\rangle,\\\n(h_v)_{v\\in K\\setminus W}\n\\right),\n\\]\nwith functions on \\(O\\)-domains. Neither input contains \\(X\\).</p><p>There are randomized local strategies on these inputs returning bilinear forms \\(F_L\\) on \\(H_i\\) and \\(F_R\\) on \\(H_i'\\), such that\n\\[\n\\Pr_i[F_R=F_L|_{H_i'\\times H_i'}]\n\\ge\n\\gamma_j:=b\\,2^{-\\ell}\\frac{\\tau^2}{2}\\,2^{-r}&gt;0.\n\\]\nEvery left output has the form\n\\[\nF_L(x,y)=z(xy),\\qquad z\\in(H_i^2)^*,\\qquad z(1)=1,\n\\]\nand satisfies\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(F_L(S_t,S_u))_{t,u}\\le1.\n\\]</p><p>The constant \\(\\gamma_j\\) depends only on \\(c,\\alpha,r,\\ell_j\\), not on \\(\\ell_i\\), branching, source alphabet, instance size, or lower bias accuracies.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 5.1–5.4 produce the two levels and the unconditional prediction inequality.</p><p>The left input determines \\(\\mathsf P\\) and \\(A\\overline M\\), because \\(AX=0\\). It therefore determines goodness and the selected witness. On good advice choose \\(z\\) uniformly from \\(z_0+Z\\), and return\n\\[\nF_L(x,y)=z(xy),\\qquad x,y\\in H_i.\n\\]\nProducts lie in \\(H\\), since \\(D_i\\) is a proper descendant of \\(D_j\\). Lemma 5.3 proves normalization and the rank-one test on \\(S\\). On bad advice use point evaluation at a fixed legal assignment. On any other well-typed input use the construction when its witness is valid, and point evaluation otherwise. No omitted lower direction enters this rule.</p><p>For the right rule, conditional on domains, projections, path, and \\(A\\), all hidden buckets have tapes independent of nonhidden buckets and every other actual matrix:\n\\[\n(h_v)_{0\\ne v\\in W}\n\\ \\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp\\\n((h_v)_{v\\notin W},(M_D)_{D\\ne D_j}).\n\\]\nThis remains true on revealing \\(E\\) and projection maps. Let \\(\\mu_{\\mathcal R}\\) be the law of \\(X\\) from these hidden buckets. It is determined by the right input: the right knows \\(O\\), the terminating cut path, spaces, and parameters. It does not require \\(E\\), projection maps, or the missing component of \\(S\\).</p><p>For each right input define\n\\[\nG_{\\mathcal R}:W\\otimes H'\\to K\n\\]\nby assembling the level-\\(i\\) right query with upper matrix \\(M_{\\mathrm{known}}+X\\), consulting its fixed label, and restoring the upper component. The lower input is precisely \\(S\\bmod\\langle a_i\\rangle\\). The extension to unused keys makes \\(G_{\\mathcal R}\\) total.</p><p>Proposition 4 gives nontrivial character bias at most \\(\\lambda\\) for \\(\\mu_{\\mathcal R}\\), before any slice conditioning. Compute Lemma 5.5’s lists for this law and function. Choose uniform \\(\\sigma\\in K^*\\). If \\(\\sigma|_W\\ne0\\) and its list is nonempty, choose uniform \\(\\Phi\\in\\mathcal H_\\sigma\\), choose deterministically \\(h_\\sigma\\in W\\) with \\(\\sigma(h_\\sigma)=1\\), and put\n\\[\nz_R=\\Phi(h_\\sigma)\\in(H')^*.\n\\]\nReturn\n\\[\nF_R(x,y)=z_R(xy)\\qquad(x,y\\in H_i').\n\\]\nOtherwise return the zero form. This is local and uses no left witness.</p><p>For analysis only, expose \\(E,O\\), all projections, path through \\(D_i\\), \\(A,a_i\\), nonhidden upper buckets, and every other matrix including full \\(S\\). Denote this record by \\(\\mathsf D\\). Do not expose hidden bucket ingredients, \\(X\\), slice or success events, or final decoder randomness.</p><p>This exposure determines the witness, while \\(X\\mid\\mathsf D\\) still has law \\(\\mu_{\\mathcal R}\\). Put\n\\[\nQ=Z|_{H'},\\qquad z'=z_0|_{H'}.\n\\]\nOn compatible good data, \\(J\\) is the nonempty affine slice\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_{\\mathsf D}=\n\\{X:X(q_a|_{H'})=v_a-M_{\\mathrm{known}}q_a\\text{ for every }a\\}.\n\\]\nThe equations specify a map on \\(Q\\), even when columns collapse on restriction. The predicted target is\n\\[\nXz'+k_0,\\qquad k_0=M_{\\mathrm{known}}z_0+u.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(p_{\\mathsf D}=\\mu_{\\mathcal R}(\\mathcal C_{\\mathsf D})\\) on good compatible data, zero otherwise. When positive, let\n\\[\nq_{\\mathsf D}\n=\n\\Pr_i[G_{\\mathcal R}(X)=Xz'+k_0\n\\mid\\mathsf D,\\ X\\in\\mathcal C_{\\mathsf D}],\n\\]\nand otherwise set it to zero. Disintegrating Lemma 5.4’s already established inequality gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_i[p_{\\mathsf D}(q_{\\mathsf D}-\\rho)]\\ge b.\n\\]\nIts integrand is nonpositive outside good compatible data with \\(p_{\\mathsf D}&gt;0\\), \\(q_{\\mathsf D}\\ge\\rho\\), and is at most one. Such favorable data therefore have probability at least \\(b\\).</p><p>For each favorable record, Lemma 5.5 supplies a pair\n\\[\n\\Phi\\in\\mathcal H_\\sigma\n\\cap\\bigl((\\sigma|_W)\\otimes z'+W^*\\otimes Q\\bigr),\n\\qquad \\sigma|_W\\ne0.\n\\]\nThe right selects one such pair with probability at least \\(2^{-\\ell}\\tau^2/2\\), and then\n\\[\nz_R=\\Phi(h_\\sigma)\\in z'+Q.\n\\]\nThe restriction of uniform \\(z\\in z_0+Z\\) is uniform on \\(z'+Q\\). Independently, the left therefore matches this particular \\(z_R\\) with probability at least \\(2^{-r}\\). Equality on \\(H'\\) gives equality of product forms on \\(H_i'\\times H_i'\\).</p><p>Averaging yields \\(\\gamma_j\\). Different compatible left witnesses certify elements of the same right-input lists, so there is no factor for their number. The constants use only \\(c,\\alpha,r,\\ell_j\\), as asserted. The right algorithm continues to use this original own-input law even after later conditioning changes the physical hidden-bucket distribution.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Selecting two successful levels — paper Section 5.1, equation (5.1).** If preliminary acceptance is at least \\(\\varepsilon\\) and \\(d\\varepsilon\\ge4\\), then some \\(i<j\\) satisfies …", "statement": "<p>**Selecting two successful levels — paper Section 5.1, equation (5.1).**</p><p>If preliminary acceptance is at least \\(\\varepsilon\\) and \\(d\\varepsilon\\ge4\\), then some \\(i&lt;j\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\Pr(\\operatorname{Acc}_i\\cap\\operatorname{Acc}_j)\n\\ge\n\\frac{d^2\\varepsilon^2-d\\varepsilon}{d(d-1)}\n\\ge3\\varepsilon^2/4.\n\\]\nIf the original-to-modified-\\(i\\) comparison error is at most \\(\\varepsilon^2/4\\), then\n\\[\n\\Pr_i(\\operatorname{Acc}_i\\cap\\operatorname{Acc}_j)\\ge c=\\varepsilon^2/2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Set \\(N=\\sum_{p=1}^d1_{\\operatorname{Acc}_p}\\). Then\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[N(N-1)]\n\\ge(\\mathbb EN)^2-\\mathbb EN\n\\ge d^2\\varepsilon^2-d\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nAverage over ordered distinct pairs of levels. No independence of acceptance events is used. Proposition 4 transfers the selected pair’s event at the lower cut, where both test levels are retained, at loss at most \\(\\varepsilon^2/4\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Quotient table and defined equality — paper Lemma 5.1, with Section 5.1 definitions.** Fix \\(i<j\\) supplied by Lemma 5.1.", "statement": "<p>**Quotient table and defined equality — paper Lemma 5.1, with Section 5.1 definitions.**</p><p>Fix \\(i&lt;j\\) supplied by Lemma 5.1. Let\n\\[\n\\mathsf P=\n(E,\\ \\text{path through }D_j,\\ (M_D)_{D\\ne D_j}).\n\\]\nThis record contains actual lifted functions, but no tag selecting the level-\\(i\\) path descendant and no projection metadata.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal D_i(D_j)\\) be all level-\\(i\\) descendants of \\(D_j\\), and let \\(s_{C,t}\\) be row \\(t\\) of \\(M_C\\). Define\n\\[\nB=B(\\mathsf P)=\n\\operatorname{span}\\left(\n\\{1\\}\\cup\n\\{s_{C,t}s_{C,u}:C\\in\\mathcal D_i(D_j),\\ 1\\le t,u\\le\\ell_i\\}\n\\right)\\subseteq H.\n\\]\nChoose a deterministic linear section \\(s:H/B\\to H\\) of the quotient map \\(\\pi:H\\to H/B\\).</p><p>For \\(\\overline M\\in K\\otimes(H/B)\\), query the fixed left labeling with upper matrix \\(s(\\overline M)\\) and all other arrays from \\(\\mathsf P\\), retaining\n\\[\nf_{\\mathsf P}(\\overline M)=\n(u,(y_C)_{C\\in\\mathcal D_i(D_j)}).\n\\]\nDefine the frozen usefulness mark\n\\[\nU(\\mathsf P,\\overline M)\n=\n1_{\\{\\Pr_i(\\operatorname{Acc}_i\\mid\\mathsf P,\\overline M)\\ge\\kappa\\}},\n\\qquad\\kappa=c/2,\n\\]\nwith value zero off support. Let \\(\\widetilde f_{\\mathsf P}\\) equal \\(f_{\\mathsf P}\\) when \\(U=1\\), and be undefined otherwise.</p><p>Resampling the level-\\(j\\) bucket in its omitted direction produces \\(M,M'\\) such that\n\\[\n\\Pr_i[\n\\widetilde f_{\\mathsf P}(\\overline M)\n=\\widetilde f_{\\mathsf P}(\\overline M')\\ne\\perp]\n\\ge c^2/8.\n\\]\nIf the enlarged upper-cut comparison error is at most \\(c^2/16\\), the same frozen tables have average defined equality at least\n\\[\n\\theta=c^2/16\n\\]\nunder the unrestricted-\\(j\\) ordinary uniform rank-one walk on \\(K\\otimes(H/B)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For an actual \\(M\\), write\n\\[\n\\overline M=\\pi(M),\\qquad\n\\Delta=M-s(\\overline M)\\in K\\otimes B.\n\\]\nProposition 3’s joint folding leaves all \\(y_C\\) unchanged and changes upper response from \\(u\\) to \\(u+\\Delta b_y\\), where\n\\[\nb_y(1)=1,\\qquad\nb_y(s_{C,t}s_{C,u})=(y_C)_t(y_C)_u.\n\\]\nJoint legality makes this functional well-defined on all pointwise relations.</p><p>The mass of \\(\\operatorname{Acc}_i\\cap\\operatorname{Acc}_j\\) with \\(U=0\\) is at most \\(\\kappa\\), by conditioning on \\((\\mathsf P,\\overline M)\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\Pr_i(\\operatorname{Acc}_j\\cap\\{U=1\\})\\ge c/2.\n\\]</p><p>Fix domains, projections, path through \\(D_i\\), all matrices other than \\(M\\), the upper omitted direction \\(a\\ne0\\), and all upper buckets except \\(h_a\\). The old and resampled \\(h_a\\) are conditionally independent with the same law. The upper right query is constant in that bucket, since \\(M\\bmod\\langle a\\rangle\\) is constant. Successful left responses therefore share all lower outputs and the upper output modulo \\(\\langle a\\rangle\\).</p><p>If \\(M'-M=a\\otimes h\\), linearity of the section gives\n\\[\n[M'-s(\\overline M')]-[M-s(\\overline M)]\n=a\\otimes(h-s(\\pi h)).\n\\]\nSuccessful samples have the same \\(b_y\\); their representative upper outputs therefore also agree modulo \\(\\langle a\\rangle\\). There are at most two useful successful representative responses. If their conditional probabilities are \\(p_1,p_2\\), independent draws match with probability at least\n\\[\np_1^2+p_2^2\\ge(p_1+p_2)^2/2.\n\\]\nAverage and apply Cauchy–Schwarz again to obtain \\(c^2/8\\).</p><p>The equality event is a function only of \\(\\mathsf P,M,M'\\) and frozen tables; it does not retain the selected lower descendant or projection metadata. Proposition 4 therefore permits the modified-\\(i\\) to unrestricted-\\(j\\) comparison with the extra bucket call.</p><p>In that reference law \\(M\\) is uniform independently of \\(\\mathsf P\\), and the bucket replacement gives \\(M'=M+a\\otimes h\\) with independent uniform \\(h\\). Quotienting by \\(B\\) gives exactly the ordinary rank-one walk on the quotient. Subtract \\(c^2/16\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Random row advice and an affine functional coset — paper Section 5.2, equations (5.6)–(5.11).** Use the frozen partial tables of Lemma 5.2 and let \\(\\mathbb P_j^0\\) be its unrestricted-\\(j\\) reference law.", "statement": "<p>**Random row advice and an affine functional coset — paper Section 5.2, equations (5.6)–(5.11).**</p><p>Use the frozen partial tables of Lemma 5.2 and let \\(\\mathbb P_j^0\\) be its unrestricted-\\(j\\) reference law. Put \\(\\eta=\\theta/2=c^2/32\\). Take \\(\\alpha,r,\\ell_0\\) from Proposition 2’s many-good-row-fibers assertion at threshold \\(\\eta\\), and assume \\(\\ell\\ge\\ell_0\\).</p><p>For uniform independent \\(A:K\\to\\mathbb F_2^r\\), advice \\((\\mathsf P,A,A\\overline M)\\) is good if its row fiber has a nonempty slice specified by at most \\(r\\) columns on which one defined response of \\(\\widetilde f_{\\mathsf P}\\) has density at least \\(\\alpha\\).</p><p>For each good advice, choose deterministically, using only that advice:\n\\[\nq_1,\\ldots,q_k\\in(H/B)^*,\\quad\nv_1,\\ldots,v_k\\in K,\\quad\nt_*=(u,(y_C)_{C\\in\\mathcal D_i(D_j)}),\n\\qquad k\\le r,\n\\]\nwitnessing goodness. Pull the \\(q_a\\) back to \\(H^*\\), and put \\(Z=\\operatorname{span}\\{q_a\\}\\subseteq\\operatorname{Ann}(B)\\). Define\n\\[\nz_0(b+s(\\overline h))=b_y(b)\n\\qquad(b\\in B,\\ \\overline h\\in H/B).\n\\]</p><p>Then good advice has probability at least\n\\[\ng=(\\eta^2/4)2^{-r\\ell}.\n\\]\nEvery \\(z\\in z_0+Z\\) satisfies\n\\[\nz(1)=1,\\qquad\n(z(s_{C,t}s_{C,u}))_{t,u}=y_Cy_C^{\\mathsf t}\n\\]\nfor every level-\\(i\\) descendant \\(C\\).</p><p>Define\n\\[\nJ=\\{\\text{advice good},\\ Mq_a=v_a\\ (1\\le a\\le k)\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nI=J\\cap\\{\\widetilde f_{\\mathsf P}(\\overline M)=t_*\\}.\n\\]\nWith \\(h_0=2^{-r\\ell}\\), \\(q_0=gh_0\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr_j^0(J)\\ge q_0,\\qquad\n\\Pr_j^0(I)\\ge\\alpha\\Pr_j^0(J).\n\\]\nBoth predicates belong to \\(\\sigma(\\mathsf P,A,\\overline M)\\). At every representative match, the actual upper left response is\n\\[\nMz_0+u.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Average conditional defined equality over \\(\\mathsf P\\). Since its average is at least \\(\\theta=2\\eta\\), the set of \\(\\mathsf P\\) with equality at least \\(\\eta\\) has probability at least\n\\[\n(\\theta-\\eta)/(1-\\eta)\\ge\\eta.\n\\]\nFor each such \\(\\mathsf P\\), Proposition 2 gives good-advice probability at least \\((\\eta/4)2^{-r\\ell}\\). Multiplying yields \\(g\\).</p><p>A selected defined response occurs on legal representative queries, so joint legality supplies \\(b_y\\in B^*\\). The decomposition\n\\[\nH=B\\oplus s(H/B)\n\\]\ndefines \\(z_0\\). For \\(M=s(\\overline M)+\\Delta\\),\n\\[\nMz_0=\\Delta b_y,\n\\]\nso joint folding gives actual response \\(Mz_0+u\\). Since every element of \\(Z\\) annihilates \\(B\\), normalization and all displayed product values persist throughout \\(z_0+Z\\).</p><p>The witness is selected from row advice alone, not the actual location in its column slice. In each good row fiber, its at most \\(r\\) columns cost at most \\(r\\ell\\) scalar equations, so have conditional probability at least \\(h_0\\). The density condition gives the two probability estimates.</p><p>Every chosen column annihilates \\(B\\), so \\(I,J\\) depend on \\(M\\) only through \\(\\overline M\\). These choices and predicates are fixed for all later law changes.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Predicting the upper response — paper Lemma 5.2.** Use Lemma 5.3’s frozen choices.", "statement": "<p>**Predicting the upper response — paper Lemma 5.2.**</p><p>Use Lemma 5.3’s frozen choices. Let \\(R_j^{(i)}\\in K\\) be the restored upper response of the level-\\(i\\) right table in the modified-\\(i\\) experiment.</p><p>If the TV comparison for observations defining \\(I,J\\) is at most\n\\[\nv\\le\\alpha q_0/16,\n\\]\nthen, with\n\\[\n\\rho=\\kappa\\alpha/4,\\qquad\nb=\\kappa\\alpha q_0/4,\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n\\Pr_i[J,\\ R_j^{(i)}=Mz_0+u]-\\rho\\Pr_i(J)\\ge b&gt;0.\n\\]\nAll constants depend only on \\(c,\\alpha,r,\\ell\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>On \\(I\\), usefulness is one. Independence of \\(A\\) and measurability give\n\\[\n\\Pr_i(I\\cap\\operatorname{Acc}_i)\n=\n\\mathbb E_i[\n1_I\\Pr_i(\\operatorname{Acc}_i\\mid\\mathsf P,\\overline M,A)]\n\\ge\\kappa\\Pr_i(I).\n\\]\nAcceptance at level \\(i\\) preserves the entire upper evaluation. Thus on \\(I\\cap\\operatorname{Acc}_i\\), the right upper response is \\(Mz_0+u\\). Exact canonical sharing permits evaluating that right response directly on \\(O\\).</p><p>Write \\(h=\\Pr_j^0(J)\\ge q_0\\). TV comparison and Lemma 5.3 give\n\\[\n\\Pr_i(I)\\ge\\alpha h-v,\\qquad\n\\Pr_i(J)\\le h+v.\n\\]\nHence the claimed left side is at least\n\\[\n(\\kappa\\alpha-\\rho)h-(\\kappa+\\rho)v\n\\ge\n\\frac{3\\kappa\\alpha}{4}q_0\n-\\kappa(1+\\alpha/4)\\frac{\\alpha q_0}{16}\n\\ge b.\n\\]\nThis is an unconditional inequality. No usefulness estimate after a finer conditioning is asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**One heavy list for all affine-slice witnesses — paper Lemma 5.3.** Let \\(K=\\mathbb F_2^\\ell\\), let \\(W\\subseteq K\\) have codimension at most \\(r\\), and let \\(H'\\) be a finite binary space.", "statement": "<p>**One heavy list for all affine-slice witnesses — paper Lemma 5.3.**</p><p>Let \\(K=\\mathbb F_2^\\ell\\), let \\(W\\subseteq K\\) have codimension at most \\(r\\), and let \\(H'\\) be a finite binary space. Put \\(V=W\\otimes H'\\), with characters\n\\[\n\\chi_\\Phi(X)=(-1)^{\\langle\\Phi,X\\rangle},\n\\qquad\n\\Phi\\in V^*=\\operatorname{Hom}(W,(H')^*).\n\\]\nSuppose a law \\(\\mu\\) satisfies\n\\[\n|\\mathbb E_\\mu\\chi_\\Phi|\\le\\lambda\\qquad(\\Phi\\ne0).\n\\]\nFix \\(G:V\\to K\\), \\(0&lt;\\rho\\le1\\), and\n\\[\nh_0=2^{-r\\ell},\\qquad \\tau=\\rho h_0/4,\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\n2^{-(\\ell-r)}&lt;\\rho/8,\\qquad\n0\\le\\lambda\\le\\min\\{h_0/2,\\tau^2/2\\}.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\sigma\\in K^*\\), put \\(g_\\sigma(X)=(-1)^{\\sigma(G(X))}\\) and\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_\\sigma=\n\\{\\Phi:|\\mathbb E_\\mu[g_\\sigma\\chi_\\Phi]|\\ge\\tau\\}.\n\\]\nEach list has size at most \\(2/\\tau^2\\).</p><p>Let \\(Q\\subseteq(H')^*\\) have dimension at most \\(r\\), and let\n\\[\n\\mathcal C=\\{X:X|_Q=t_Q\\}\n\\]\nbe a nonempty affine slice, with \\(t_Q\\in\\operatorname{Hom}(Q,W)\\). If, for \\(z'\\in(H')^*\\), \\(k_0\\in K\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr_\\mu[G(X)=Xz'+k_0\\mid X\\in\\mathcal C]\\ge\\rho,\n\\]\nthen some \\(\\sigma\\) with \\(\\sigma|_W\\ne0\\) has a list element in\n\\[\n(\\sigma|_W)\\otimes z'+W^*\\otimes Q.\n\\]\nThe lists depend on \\(\\mu,G,W,H',\\tau\\), not on \\(Q,t_Q,z',k_0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(w=\\dim W\\), \\(q=\\dim Q\\). The restriction map\n\\[\nV\\to\\operatorname{Hom}(Q,W)\n\\]\nis surjective by extending a basis of \\(Q\\) to \\((H')^*\\). The slice equations have rank \\(wq\\le\\ell r\\), with dual space \\(L=W^*\\otimes Q\\). For \\(X_0\\in\\mathcal C\\),\n\\[\n1_{\\mathcal C}(X)\n=\\frac1{|L|}\\sum_{\\Psi\\in L}\\chi_\\Psi(X_0)\\chi_\\Psi(X).\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\mu(\\mathcal C)\\ge2^{-wq}-\\lambda\n\\ge h_0-\\lambda\\ge h_0/2&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>Expand equality over \\(K^*\\):\n\\[\n\\Pr_\\mu[G(X)=Xz'+k_0\\mid\\mathcal C]\n=\n2^{-\\ell}\\sum_{\\sigma\\in K^*}\n(-1)^{\\sigma(k_0)}\n\\mathbb E_\\mu[\ng_\\sigma\\chi_{(\\sigma|_W)\\otimes z'}\\mid\\mathcal C].\n\\]\nThe fraction of \\(\\sigma\\) annihilating \\(W\\) is \\(2^{-w}\\le2^{-(\\ell-r)}&lt;\\rho/8\\). Each term has absolute value at most one, so some remaining \\(\\sigma\\) has\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\mathbb E_\\mu[\ng_\\sigma\\chi_{(\\sigma|_W)\\otimes z'}\\mid\\mathcal C]\n\\right|\\ge\\rho/2.\n\\]\nMultiply by the slice mass and insert the character expansion. Its coefficients have total absolute value one, so some \\(\\Psi\\in L\\) gives correlation at least\n\\[\n(\\rho/2)(h_0/2)=\\tau\n\\]\nat \\((\\sigma|_W)\\otimes z'+\\Psi\\). The offset contributes only a sign.</p><p>For the list bound, choose distinct \\(\\Phi_1,\\ldots,\\Phi_m\\) from one list and signs \\(\\epsilon_a\\) aligning their correlations. Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\nm^2\\tau^2\n\\le\n\\left(\\mathbb E_\\mu[\ng_\\sigma\\sum_a\\epsilon_a\\chi_{\\Phi_a}]\n\\right)^2\n\\le\n\\mathbb E_\\mu\\left(\\sum_a\\epsilon_a\\chi_{\\Phi_a}\\right)^2\n\\le m+m(m-1)\\lambda\n\\le m+m^2\\lambda.\n\\]\nFor distinct indices, the product character is nonzero, justifying the off-diagonal bound. If \\(m&gt;0\\), \\(\\lambda\\le\\tau^2/2\\) implies \\(m\\le2/\\tau^2\\). All correlations are unconditional under the original law \\(\\mu\\); no ambient-dimension factor occurs.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**From tested rank to full rank — paper Proposition 6.3.** Fix \\(i<j\\) and the original randomized decoders of Proposition 5, with agreement at least \\(\\gamma=\\gamma_j>0\\) under the modified-\\(i\\) law.", "statement": "<p>**From tested rank to full rank — paper Proposition 6.3.**</p><p>Fix \\(i&lt;j\\) and the original randomized decoders of Proposition 5, with agreement at least \\(\\gamma=\\gamma_j&gt;0\\) under the modified-\\(i\\) law. Put\n\\[\nH_i=H_{D_i}(E),\\qquad\nH_i'=H_{D_i}(O)\\subseteq H_i,\\qquad\nK_i=\\mathbb F_2^{\\ell_i},\\qquad\nS=M_{D_i}.\n\\]\nFor a bilinear form \\(F\\) on \\(H_i\\), write\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Gram}_F(S)=(F(S_u,S_v))_{u,v}.\n\\]\nCall \\(F\\) admissible at \\(S\\) if\n\\[\nF(x,y)=z(xy),\\qquad\nz\\in(H_i^2)^*,\\qquad z(1)=1,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{rank}\\operatorname{Gram}_F(S)\\le1.\n\\]</p><p>Apply Proposition 2’s inverse theorem at threshold\n\\[\n\\eta_{\\mathrm{low}}=\\gamma^2/8\n\\]\nand let its constants be \\(\\alpha'&gt;0,r',\\ell_0'\\). Choose integers \\(m,s\\ge1\\) and \\(\\ell_i\\) with\n\\[\n[1+(2^m-1)^2]2^{-m^2}&lt;\\alpha'/2,\n\\]\n\\[\n2^{m-(s-2r')}&lt;\\alpha'/2,\n\\qquad\n\\ell_i\\ge\\max\\{\\ell_0',2m+r'\\}.\n\\]\nLet \\(L_i\\) bound the enlarged raw child blocks including the rows of \\(S\\) and one extra independent row. Take \\(n_i\\) sufficiently large that\n\\[\n\\frac{2\\beta_iL_i}{1-\\beta_i}\\le\\gamma^2/64,\n\\qquad\n\\upsilon_i\\le\\gamma^2/64,\n\\qquad\n\\beta_i=n_i^{-2/3},\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\upsilon_i\\) is their modified-to-unrestricted TV bound.</p><p>Then\n\\[\n\\Pr_i[\nF_L|_{H_i'\\times H_i'}=F_R,\\ \nF_L\\text{ admissible at }S,\\ \n\\operatorname{rank}F_L\\le s]\n\\ge\\gamma/2.\n\\]</p><p>The rank cutoff and row requirements depend only on \\(\\gamma\\). The likelihood and TV requirements can be met after fixing source alphabet and lower branching, independently of higher branching.</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose high-rank agreement, with left rank greater than \\(s\\), has probability at least \\(\\gamma/2\\). Fix complete local tapes preserving that unconditional mass, and erase inadmissible or rank-at-most-\\(s\\) left outputs. The nonerased agreement probability is \\(h\\ge\\gamma/2\\).</p><p>Lemma 6.1 and the error assumptions give\n\\[\n\\Pr_{\\mathrm{unif}}[f(S)=f(S+a_il)\\ne\\perp]\n\\ge\n\\gamma^2/4-\\gamma^2/64-\\gamma^2/64\n=7\\gamma^2/32&gt;\\eta_{\\mathrm{low}}.\n\\]\nFix domains and independent background data at a value with equality probability at least \\(\\eta_{\\mathrm{low}}\\). The remaining \\(S,l,a_i\\) retain their independent uniform laws.</p><p>Proposition 2 selects one fixed defined output form \\(F\\) and a nonempty row/column slice, with at most \\(r'\\) specifications of each kind, on which \\(f(S)=F\\) has density at least \\(\\alpha'\\). Every such occurrence has\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}F&gt;s,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{rank}\\operatorname{Gram}_F(S)\\le1.\n\\]\nOnly now fix this output and apply Lemma 6.2. The chosen \\(m,s,\\ell_i\\) make the latter Gram-rank event have density strictly less than \\(\\alpha'\\), a contradiction.</p><p>Dependence of the original witness and \\(B(S)\\) on \\(S\\) does not enter: each occurrence of the selected fixed form passes its own displayed-row test.</p><p>Thus high-rank agreement has probability less than \\(\\gamma/2\\) for the original randomized decoders. Their total agreement is at least \\(\\gamma\\), and every left output is admissible by Proposition 5. The low-rank mass is at least \\(\\gamma/2\\). Tape fixing was used only for contradiction, not to replace the conclusion’s algorithms.</p><p>Choose \\(m\\), then \\(s\\), then \\(\\ell_i\\), all from \\(\\gamma\\). Proposition 4 makes the remaining errors small by increasing \\(n_i\\), after source alphabet and lower branching are fixed. For finitely many upper levels, take all row requirements and the maximum rank cutoff.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Resampling and transfer to full equality — paper Lemma 6.1.** Use the modified-\\(i\\) setting of Proposition 6.", "statement": "<p>**Resampling and transfer to full equality — paper Lemma 6.1.**</p><p>Use the modified-\\(i\\) setting of Proposition 6. Fix complete local random tapes, so both decoders are deterministic functions of their prescribed inputs. Erase a left output if it is not admissible at its own input or has full rank at most \\(s\\).</p><p>Suppose nonerased left-right agreement on \\(H_i'\\times H_i'\\) has probability \\(h\\). Let \\(L_i,\\upsilon_i\\) be the enlarged likelihood and TV bounds of Proposition 6.</p><p>There is an unrestricted experiment with independent uniform\n\\[\nS\\in K_i\\otimes H_i,\\qquad\nl\\in H_i,\\qquad\na_i\\in K_i\\setminus\\{0\\},\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\n\\Pr_{\\mathrm{unif}}[\nf(S)=f(S+a_il)\\ne\\perp]\n\\ge\nh^2-\\frac{2\\beta_iL_i}{1-\\beta_i}-\\upsilon_i.\n\\]\nThe partial table \\(f\\) depends on background data independent of \\((S,a_i,l)\\), conditional on domains. For every fixed background it is one table on \\(K_i\\otimes H_i\\), with no \\(a_i\\) or \\(l\\) input. Every defined output is admissible at its argument and has full rank greater than \\(s\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>At the modified cut, all advice outside \\(S\\) is generated by independent exterior ingredients, uniform descendant arrays, and separate uniform scalar cut calls. Ancestor arrays are deterministic functions of those calls. The rows of \\(S\\) use independent tapes. The full cut-row count is \\(\\widehat T_i\\); the resampling adds one row.</p><p>First expose a record \\(\\mathsf C\\) containing both domains, projections, path through \\(D_i\\), \\(A\\), exterior ingredients, descendant arrays, all cut calls other than \\(S\\), and the fixed tapes. Conditional on \\((\\mathsf C,a_i)\\), \\(S\\) is uniform in \\(K_i\\otimes H_i'\\).</p><p>Write \\(q=S\\bmod a_i\\). Once \\((\\mathsf C,a_i,q)\\) is fixed, the right answer \\(F_R\\) is fixed. Let\n\\[\np(\\mathsf C,a_i,q)\n=\n\\Pr[f(S)\\ne\\perp,\\ f(S)|_{H_i'\\times H_i'}=F_R\n\\mid\\mathsf C,a_i,q].\n\\]\nDraw \\(S,S'\\) independently from that quotient fiber. Then\n\\[\n\\Pr[\\text{both nonerased draws agree with }F_R]\n=\\mathbb E[p^2]\\ge(\\mathbb Ep)^2=h^2.\n\\]\nIn particular their restrictions agree with probability at least \\(h^2\\).</p><p>Before inspecting agreement, this pair law is exactly:\n\\[\n\\mathsf C\\text{ has its original law},\\quad a_i\\text{ uniform nonzero},\n\\]\n\\[\nS\\text{ uniform in }K_i\\otimes H_i',\\quad\nl\\text{ independently uniform in }H_i',\\quad\nS'=S+a_il.\n\\]\nIndeed, for fixed \\(S\\), \\(l\\mapsto S+a_il\\) bijects \\(H_i'\\) with its quotient fiber. No success or rank filtering has been introduced.</p><p>For a second exposure, retain \\(E\\), independent exterior ingredients, path through \\(D_i\\), \\(A\\), fixed tapes, and \\(a_i\\). In each child retain only lifted raw blocks, including rows of \\(S,l\\), and descendant arrays. Do not expose \\(O\\), marks, projection metadata, right output, or success.</p><p>Both full left outputs are functions of these permitted data. For applying Proposition 4’s posterior estimate, replace an undefined output by the zero form. It gives\n\\[\n\\Pr[\nF_0,F_1\\ne\\perp,\\ F_0\\ne F_1,\\\nF_0|_{H_i'\\times H_i'}=F_1|_{H_i'\\times H_i'}]\n\\le\\frac{2\\beta_iL_i}{1-\\beta_i},\n\\]\nwhere \\(F_0=f(S)\\), \\(F_1=f(S+a_il)\\), and definedness refers to the original partial outputs.</p><p>Subtract this unconditional bad-event bound from the \\(h^2\\) restriction-agreement event. Full defined equality then transfers, by Proposition 4’s sparse comparison with the extra row, at additional loss \\(\\upsilon_i\\).</p><p>In the unrestricted law, \\(S,l\\) and all other cut calls are independent uniforms in their unprojected spaces, and \\(a_i\\) is independent. Fixing remaining data leaves one partial table \\(f(S)\\).</p><p>At arbitrary \\(S\\), run the original left algorithm on its reconstructed input and then apply local erasure. Its \\(B(S)\\), quotient section, goodness predicate, and upper witness are recomputed from that input. Frozen rules are not frozen values. Neither \\(a_i\\) nor \\(l\\) is supplied, and outside original support the same local extension and admissibility erasure apply.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Fixed forms on affine row/column slices — paper Lemma 6.2.** Let \\(H\\) be a finite binary space and \\(F\\) a fixed bilinear form on \\(H\\).", "statement": "<p>**Fixed forms on affine row/column slices — paper Lemma 6.2.**</p><p>Let \\(H\\) be a finite binary space and \\(F\\) a fixed bilinear form on \\(H\\). Let \\(m\\ge1\\), \\(r'\\ge0\\), and \\(\\ell\\ge2m+r'\\). Let\n\\[\n\\mathcal A=\n\\{S\\in H^\\ell:A_0S=U,\\ Sq_v=w_v\\ (1\\le v\\le b)\\}\n\\]\nbe a nonempty affine slice, where \\(A_0\\) has at most \\(r'\\) rows and \\(b\\le r'\\), with \\(q_v\\in H^*\\).</p><p>For uniform \\(S\\in\\mathcal A\\), putting \\(t_0=\\operatorname{rank}F-2r'\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr[\\operatorname{rank}\\operatorname{Gram}_F(S)\\le1]\n\\le\n(2^m-1)2^{-t_0}\n+[1+(2^m-1)^2]2^{-m^2}.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\operatorname{rank}F&gt;s\\), the first term may be replaced by\n\\[\n2^{m-(s-2r')}.\n\\]\nNo symmetry or nonalternation assumption is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>Remove dependent row equations, leaving \\(a=\\operatorname{rank}A_0\\le r'\\). Extend those row combinations to an invertible \\(\\ell\\times\\ell\\) matrix \\(T\\), and put \\(Y=TS\\). Its first \\(a\\) rows are fixed, and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Gram}_F(Y)\n=T\\operatorname{Gram}_F(S)T^{\\mathsf t}.\n\\]\nThus Gram rank is unchanged. This transforms rows of a fixed form, not a decoder-selected rule.</p><p>Remove dependent column functionals, leaving a basis \\(q_1,\\ldots,q_c\\), \\(c\\le r'\\), and put\n\\[\nN=\\bigcap_{v=1}^c\\ker q_v.\n\\]\nNonemptiness supplies compatibility of all prescribed dependencies. After fixing the first \\(a\\) rows, the remaining \\(\\ell-a\\ge2m\\) rows are independent uniforms on possibly different affine translates \\(u_k+N\\).</p><p>Restriction in the first argument to \\(N\\) loses at most \\(c\\) rank; restriction of the second dual to \\(N^*\\) loses at most another \\(c\\). Hence\n\\[\nt:=\\operatorname{rank}(F|_{N\\times N})\n\\ge\\operatorname{rank}F-2c\\ge t_0.\n\\]</p><p>Choose two disjoint groups of \\(m\\) free rows \\(x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\) and \\(y_1,\\ldots,y_m\\). Write \\(x_u=u_u+n_u\\), with independent uniform \\(n_u\\in N\\). Then\n\\[\n\\lambda_u=F(x_u,\\cdot)|_N\n\\]\nare independent uniforms on specified translates of the \\(t\\)-dimensional image of \\(F|_{N\\times N}\\) in \\(N^*\\).</p><p>For each nonzero \\(e\\in\\mathbb F_2^m\\), \\(\\sum_ue_u\\lambda_u\\) is uniform on a translate of that image, so its probability of zero is either zero or \\(2^{-t}\\). A union bound gives\n\\[\n\\Pr[\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_m\\text{ dependent}]\n\\le(2^m-1)2^{-t}.\n\\]\nThe affine offsets are included in these full functionals.</p><p>Conditional on their independence, the map\n\\[\nN\\to\\mathbb F_2^m,\\qquad\nn\\mapsto(\\lambda_1(n),\\ldots,\\lambda_m(n))\n\\]\nis surjective. Each independent second-group row therefore gives a uniform cross column. Thus\n\\[\n(F(x_u,y_v))_{u,v=1}^m\n\\]\nis a uniform binary \\(m\\times m\\) matrix.</p><p>Exactly one matrix has rank zero, and exactly \\((2^m-1)^2\\) have rank one: the factorizations \\(uv^{\\mathsf t}\\) with \\(u,v\\ne0\\) are unique over \\(\\mathbb F_2\\). A Gram matrix of rank at most one has a cross submatrix of rank at most one. Add the dependence error to this uniform-matrix probability and use \\(t\\ge t_0\\).</p><p>If \\(\\operatorname{rank}F&gt;s\\), then\n\\[\n(2^m-1)2^{-t}\\le2^{m-(s-2r')}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Clean children and independent repetition — paper Proposition 7.4.** Fix \\(i<j\\), the modified-\\(i\\) experiment, and the original randomized decoders of Proposition 5.", "statement": "<p>**Clean children and independent repetition — paper Proposition 7.4.**</p><p>Fix \\(i&lt;j\\), the modified-\\(i\\) experiment, and the original randomized decoders of Proposition 5. Let \\(s\\ge1\\), and assume\n\\[\n\\operatorname{val}(G_{\\mathrm{src}})&lt;\\frac1{2s^2}.\n\\]\nWrite\n\\[\nH_i=H_{D_i}(E),\\qquad H_i'=H_{D_i}(O).\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal L_{i,j,s}\\) be the event that\n\\[\nF_R=F_L|_{H_i'\\times H_i'},\n\\]\nwhile \\(F_L\\) is a normalized product form\n\\[\nF_L(x,y)=z(xy),\\qquad z(1)=1,\n\\]\nof full rank at most \\(s\\).</p><p>Choose the first leaf of each child of \\(D_i\\) as its designated leaf. Stop every ancestor scalar call at \\(D_i\\), including every bucket of \\(M_{D_j}\\), before selecting visible buckets, and include the \\(\\ell_i\\) rows of \\(S=M_{D_i}\\). Pad with unused independent calls to obtain the fixed count\n\\[\nT_i^{\\mathrm{cl}}=\\widehat T_i\n=\n\\ell_i+\\sum_{h=i+1}^d(2^{\\ell_h}-1)\n\\prod_{p=i+1}^h(2R_p).\n\\]</p><p>For each cut call \\(q\\), represent its uniform value as\n\\[\nU_q=\\sum_{C\\text{ child of }D_i}U_{q,C},\n\\]\nwith independent uniform\n\\[\nU_{q,C}\\in H_C(O)^2.\n\\]\nCall a child \\(C\\) clean if it is projected, every \\(U_{q,C}=0\\), and every sampled array at a nonleaf in its subtree is zero. Let \\(\\mathcal C\\) be the set of clean children.</p><p>There is a constant \\(p_{0,i}&gt;0\\), independent of instance, \\(n_i\\), and higher branching, such that\n\\[\n\\Pr(\\mathcal L_{i,j,s})\n\\le\n(1-\\beta_ip_{0,i}/64)^{n_i}\n\\le\n\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{p_{0,i}}{64}n_i^{1/3}\\right).\n\\]\nIt is uniform over fixed labelings and randomized decoders.</p><p>The constant depends only on the source power, fixed row and product counts, and branching below \\(i\\). It is given by the zero probability in Lemma 7.2.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 7.1 and the source-value assumption give\n\\[\n\\operatorname{val}(G_{\\mathrm{odd},s})&lt;1/2.\n\\]\nFix \\(\\mathsf D_{\\mathrm{pub}}=d_0\\), and put \\(k=|\\mathcal C|\\). Lemma 7.2 leaves exactly \\(k\\) independent source edges. Lemma 7.3 gives local strategies for their odd-list repeated game and an inclusion of \\(\\mathcal L_{i,j,s}\\) in acceptance of all coordinates.</p><p>Proposition 1’s repetition bound with \\(g=1/2\\) gives\n\\[\n\\Pr[\\mathcal L_{i,j,s}\\mid\\mathsf D_{\\mathrm{pub}}=d_0]\n\\le(63/64)^k.\n\\]\nFor \\(k=0\\), use one. Independent decoder randomness is handled by fixing complete tapes, applying the deterministic bound, and averaging.</p><p>Average over public records. Conditional on \\(\\Xi\\), Lemma 7.2 gives independent clean indicators of probability \\(\\beta_ip_{0,i}\\), so\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E[(63/64)^{|\\mathcal C|}\\mid\\Xi]\n&amp;=\n\\prod_{C\\text{ child of }D_i}\n(1-\\beta_ip_{0,i}+(63/64)\\beta_ip_{0,i})\\\\\n&amp;=(1-\\beta_ip_{0,i}/64)^{n_i}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSince \\(n_i\\beta_i=n_i^{1/3}\\), \\(1-x\\le e^{-x}\\) gives the exponential bound.</p><p>As in paper Remark 7.5, independent exterior recipe choices may use \\(T_i^{\\mathrm{cl}}(\\xi)\\le T_i^{\\mathrm{cl}}\\) calls. Their exact zero probabilities \\(p_{0,C}(\\xi)\\ge p_{0,i}\\) remain question-independent. The exact factors cancel in the conditional source-law argument, and\n\\[\n\\Pr(\\mathcal L_{i,j,s})\n\\le\n\\mathbb E_\\Xi\\prod_C\n(1-\\beta_ip_{0,C}(\\Xi)/64)\n\\le\n\\exp(-p_{0,i}n_i^{1/3}/64).\n\\]\nOnly the last inequality uses the uniform lower bound.</p><p>Once source power and lower branching are fixed, taking\n\\[\nn_i^{1/3}&gt;\n\\frac{64}{p_{0,i}}\\log\\frac4{\\gamma_j}\n\\]\nmakes this bound less than \\(\\gamma_j/4\\). Finitely many \\(j&gt;i\\) can be accommodated simultaneously. Higher branching changes only exterior expressions, not call counts or clean child dimensions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Odd-list projection and extraction — paper Lemma 7.1.** For a finite nonempty set \\(\\Lambda\\), define \\( \\mathcal O_s(\\Lambda) = \\{L\\subseteq\\Lambda:1\\le|L|\\le s,\\ |L|\\text{ odd}\\}. \\) For a map …", "statement": "<p>**Odd-list projection and extraction — paper Lemma 7.1.**</p><p>For a finite nonempty set \\(\\Lambda\\), define\n\\[\n\\mathcal O_s(\\Lambda)\n=\n\\{L\\subseteq\\Lambda:1\\le|L|\\le s,\\ |L|\\text{ odd}\\}.\n\\]\nFor a map \\(\\pi:\\Lambda\\to\\Gamma\\), define\n\\[\n\\pi_{\\mathrm{odd}}(L)\n=\n\\{b\\in\\Gamma:|L\\cap\\pi^{-1}(b)|\\text{ odd}\\}.\n\\]\nThen \\(\\pi_{\\mathrm{odd}}\\) maps \\(\\mathcal O_s(\\Lambda)\\) into \\(\\mathcal O_s(\\Gamma)\\).</p><p>Let \\(G_{\\mathrm{odd},s}\\) have the source questions and edge distribution, with these odd-list answer sets and parity-pushforward edge maps. It is a total projection game, and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{val}(G_{\\mathrm{odd},s})\n\\le s^2\\operatorname{val}(G_{\\mathrm{src}}).\n\\]</p><p>Suppose\n\\[\nF(x,y)=z(xy),\\qquad z\\in(H_i^2)^*,\\qquad z(1)=1,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{rank}F\\le s.\n\\]\nAt every leaf \\(\\lambda\\), let \\(e_a\\) be the indicator of local legal answer \\(a\\). The diagonal support\n\\[\n\\{a:F(e_a,e_a)=1\\}\n\\]\nbelongs to \\(\\mathcal O_s(\\Lambda_\\lambda)\\). If that leaf is projected by \\(\\pi_e\\) and \\(F'\\) is the restriction of \\(F\\), its right diagonal support is the parity pushforward of the left support.</p>", "proof": "<p>Parity gives\n\\[\n|\\pi_{\\mathrm{odd}}(L)|\\equiv|L|\\equiv1\\pmod2.\n\\]\nEach retained right answer has at least one left preimage in \\(L\\), and these preimage sets are disjoint. Thus the right support is nonempty, odd, and of size at most \\(|L|\\le s\\).</p><p>For any deterministic odd-list labeling, independently select uniform elements of the two returned lists. On an accepted list edge, the lists contain an accepting ordinary pair, selected with probability at least\n\\[\n1/(|L||R|)\\ge s^{-2}.\n\\]\nThis is a legal randomized source strategy, whose success is at most source value by Proposition 1. Maximize over list labelings.</p><p>For extraction,\n\\[\ne_ae_b=0\\ (a\\ne b),\\qquad e_a^2=e_a,\\qquad\n\\sum_ae_a=1.\n\\]\nAll indicators lie in \\(H_i\\). Hence the matrix \\((F(e_a,e_b))_{a,b}\\) is diagonal, with diagonal \\(d_a=z(e_a)\\). Its rank is the integer support size, at most \\(\\operatorname{rank}F\\le s\\). Also\n\\[\n\\sum_ad_a=z(1)=1\n\\]\nin \\(\\mathbb F_2\\), so the support is odd and nonempty.</p><p>For a projected answer \\(b\\), its pulled-back indicator is\n\\[\ne_b'=\\sum_{\\pi_e(a)=b}e_a.\n\\]\nAgreement and the diagonal identity give\n\\[\nF'(e_b',e_b')=\\sum_{\\pi_e(a)=b}d_a.\n\\]\nThis is precisely parity pushforward.</p><p>To make extraction total on every decoder output, return its diagonal support if that support is odd and of size at most \\(s\\); otherwise return the singleton containing the first legal answer in a fixed order. Each test and default uses only the player’s own question and output.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Exact clean probability and conditional source law — paper Lemma 7.2.** Use Proposition 7’s padded call construction.", "statement": "<p>**Exact clean probability and conditional source law — paper Lemma 7.2.**</p><p>Use Proposition 7’s padded call construction. Let \\(\\Xi\\) record:</p><p class=\"li\">questions outside \\(D_i\\);</p><p class=\"li\">the path through \\(D_i\\);</p><p class=\"li\">exterior ingredients of stopped expressions;</p><p class=\"li\">arrays supported outside \\(D_i\\);</p><p class=\"li\">independent choices \\(A,a_i\\).</p><p>Exterior functions are recorded on their supporting subtree domains with expression locations. Assembled ancestors are not independently exposed.</p><p>Sample a complete source edge at every leaf, using its projection only when that child is marked. Write \\(\\mu\\) for the source-edge law and \\(e_C\\) for a child’s tuple of \\(m_i\\) leaf edges, with law \\(\\mu^{\\otimes m_i}\\).</p><p>Conditional on any positive-probability \\(\\Xi=\\xi\\), clean indicators are independent, each with probability \\(\\beta_ip_{0,i}\\), where\n\\[\np_{0,i}\n=\n2^{\n-T_i^{\\mathrm{cl}}\\dim(H_C'^2)\n-\\sum_{\\substack{D\\preceq C\\\\D\\text{ nonleaf}}}\n\\ell_{\\operatorname{level}(D)}\\dim H_D'\n}\n&gt;0.\n\\]\nHere \\(H_D'\\) is the space on a fully projected subtree of the specified shape, and \\(D\\preceq C\\) includes \\(D=C\\). This exponent is identical for all children and all source questions and projection positions.</p><p>After fixing \\(\\mathcal C\\), expose all source questions, projection choices, and latent data of nonclean children, and all source edge records at nondesignated leaves of clean children. Denote this exposure, together with \\(\\Xi,\\mathcal C\\), by \\(\\mathsf D_{\\mathrm{pub}}\\).</p><p>Conditional on every positive-probability value of \\(\\mathsf D_{\\mathrm{pub}}\\), the designated clean leaf edge records are independent, each with distribution \\(\\mu\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Conditional on \\(\\Xi\\), source samples inside \\(D_i\\) remain independent, since its exterior ingredients are supported outside that subtree and use independent randomness.</p><p>Fix a child edge tuple \\(e_C\\). Conditional on projection, its \\(T_i^{\\mathrm{cl}}\\) latent summands are independent uniforms in \\(H_C'^2\\), all zero with probability\n\\[\n2^{-T_i^{\\mathrm{cl}}\\dim(H_C'^2)}.\n\\]\nAn independent uniform \\(\\ell_p\\)-row array in \\(H_D'\\) is zero with probability\n\\[\n2^{-\\ell_p\\dim H_D'}.\n\\]\nMultiply these probabilities. Each latent summand is set to zero separately; possible cancellations of nonzero summands and shared constants are irrelevant.</p><p>The resulting probability is exactly independent of \\(e_C\\). Every fully projected leaf domain is \\(\\mathbb F_2^t\\), with separate factors even for repeated IDs. Coordinatewise product bijections between fully projected subtrees preserve full leaf function spaces, sums, and pointwise products. Induction identifies all corresponding \\(H_D'\\) and \\(H_D'^2\\), hence their dimensions. IDs and selected positions do not enter.</p><p>Thus, for every child tuple \\(e\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr[e_C=e,\\ C\\in\\mathcal C\\mid\\Xi=\\xi]\n=\n\\mu^{\\otimes m_i}(e)\\,\\beta_ip_{0,i}.\n\\]\nChild edge tuples, flags, and latent blocks are independent across children. Clean indicators are therefore independent, and conditioning on cleanliness cancels the constant factor, leaving the original product edge law.</p><p>Fixing the entire clean mask preserves a product across children. Exposing nonclean data does not further constrain clean tuples. Within each clean tuple, exposing nondesignated edges leaves its designated edge with law \\(\\mu\\). Natural ID collisions remain permitted and impose no extra equality constraints.</p><p>The count \\(T_i^{\\mathrm{cl}}\\) uses row and product counts but no branching; the child dimensions use only branching below \\(i\\). This proves the asserted parameter independence. For \\(i=1\\), the descendant-array sum is empty.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Local reconstruction on clean children — paper Lemma 7.3.** Fix a positive-probability public record \\(\\mathsf D_{\\mathrm{pub}}=d_0\\) as in Lemma 7.2.", "statement": "<p>**Local reconstruction on clean children — paper Lemma 7.3.**</p><p>Fix a positive-probability public record \\(\\mathsf D_{\\mathrm{pub}}=d_0\\) as in Lemma 7.2.</p><p>From \\(d_0\\) and its own designated source questions in the clean children, each player can reconstruct exactly its prescribed input to the original decoder of Proposition 5.</p><p>Composing those decoders with the total diagonal extraction rule of Lemma 7.1 gives local strategies for\n\\[\nG_{\\mathrm{odd},s}^{\\otimes|\\mathcal C|}.\n\\]\nWhenever original decoded forms agree on \\(H_i'\\times H_i'\\) and the left form is a normalized product form of full rank at most \\(s\\), these strategies accept every designated clean edge.</p>", "proof": "<p>The left combines its designated clause-tuple questions with exposed questions to reconstruct \\(E\\), without needing the designated projection positions or right IDs. The right combines its designated variable-tuple questions with the exposure to reconstruct \\(O\\), without needing the designated clause tuples. Repeated IDs remain separate slots.</p><p>At nonclean children, the exposure supplies actual latent summands and descendant arrays, including projections for left pullback. At clean children, substitute zero symbols indexed only by call, row, and tree location. Each player interprets these as zero functions on its own domain; no partner-domain metadata is carried, and zero pulls back to zero.</p><p>Sum child contributions to reconstruct all cut calls, including rows of \\(S\\), and evaluate stopped expressions using exposed exterior ingredients. This reconstructs ancestor arrays and their individual buckets, hence all required visible buckets. It uses the sampled decomposition, not a claimed unique decomposition.</p><p>Functions ignoring all clean arguments form an algebra closed under sums and products. Reconstructed cut calls lie in that algebra, and exterior ingredients are supported outside \\(D_i\\). Induction through stopped expressions shows that reconstructed ancestors ignore every clean argument. Descendant arrays in clean children are zero. Thus missing partner domains do not enter.</p><p>Each player has its own domains and the required functions. It can form canonical keys, consult the fixed labeling, and restore outputs. Clean slots disappear under support reduction; nonclean projected slots have exposed pullback data. Hypothetical matrices used by a decoder can be constructed on its own known domain even when they use designated arguments.</p><p>The simulation omits extra public information from decoder arguments: the right receives only \\(S\\bmod a_i\\), even if full \\(S\\) is publicly reconstructible; the left receives no \\(a_i\\); neither receives hidden upper inputs absent from its original input.</p><p>Run the same fixed algorithms. In particular, the right still computes heavy lists using the original reference law determined by its own input, not the physical law after clean conditioning. No small-bias statement after cleanliness is needed.</p><p>Finally, total diagonal extraction is a legal local rule at designated source questions. On normalized low-rank agreement, Lemma 7.1 makes the left lists valid and the right lists their parity pushforwards. All designated edges accept. This is an event inclusion, not conditioning on agreement, validity, or rank.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Parameter selection and preliminary soundness — paper Proposition 8.1.** For each fixed rational \\(\\delta\\in(0,1)\\), set \\( \\varepsilon=\\delta/3. \\) The parameters of Propositions 3–7 can be chosen as constants …", "statement": "<p>**Parameter selection and preliminary soundness — paper Proposition 8.1.**</p><p>For each fixed rational \\(\\delta\\in(0,1)\\), set\n\\[\n\\varepsilon=\\delta/3.\n\\]\nThe parameters of Propositions 3–7 can be chosen as constants depending only on \\(\\delta\\), in the following order:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Choose depth \\(d\\) with \\(d\\varepsilon\\ge4\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Fix upper inverse constants from\n\\[\nc=\\varepsilon^2/2,\\qquad\n\\kappa=c/2,\\qquad\n\\eta=c^2/32,\n\\]\nusing Proposition 2 at threshold \\(\\eta/2\\), and put \\(\\rho=\\kappa\\alpha/4\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Choose row counts and product counts from the root downward, meeting upper decoding requirements and all previously fixed lower rank-test requirements.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Take one common rank cutoff \\(s\\) dominating all pairwise cutoffs.</p><p class=\"li num\">5. Choose the source power \\(t\\) so that on unsatisfiable inputs\n\\[\n\\operatorname{val}(G_{\\mathrm{src}})&lt;1/(2s^2).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">6. Choose branching numbers \\(n_1,\\ldots,n_d\\), each a cube greater than one, from the leaves upward, meeting all TV, posterior, and clean-repetition tolerances.</p><p>With these parameters, the preliminary weighted legal-answer game has:</p><p class=\"li\">perfect completeness;</p><p class=\"li\">projection fibers of size at most two;</p><p class=\"li\">value strictly less than \\(\\varepsilon\\) on every unsatisfiable input.</p>", "proof": "<p>For each level \\(j\\), once \\(\\ell_j\\) is chosen define\n\\[\nh_j=2^{-r\\ell_j},\\qquad\ng_j=(\\eta^2/4)2^{-r\\ell_j},\\qquad\nq_j=g_jh_j,\n\\]\n\\[\nb_j=\\kappa\\alpha q_j/4,\\qquad\n\\tau_j=\\rho h_j/4,\\qquad\n\\gamma_j=b_j2^{-\\ell_j-r}\\tau_j^2/2.\n\\]</p><p>Choose \\(\\ell_j\\) from the root downward. When choosing it, all \\(\\gamma_k\\) for \\(k&gt;j\\) are fixed. For each such \\(k\\), use Proposition 6 to choose lower inverse constants, cross-matrix size, and rank cutoff from \\(\\gamma_k\\). Make \\(\\ell_j\\) meet all those finitely many row requirements and\n\\[\n\\ell_j\\ge\\ell_0,\\qquad\n2^{-(\\ell_j-r)}&lt;\\rho/8.\n\\]\nAt the root there are no preceding lower-test requirements.</p><p>Choose\n\\[\n\\lambda_j\\le\\min\\{h_j/2,\\tau_j^2/2\\},\n\\qquad\n(7/8)^{R_j}\\le\\lambda_j,\n\\]\nand \\(R_j\\ge R_{\\min}\\). Proposition 4’s scalar induction needs only the common quarter-balance minimum below \\(j\\), so this creates no dependence on lower accuracies or source dimensions.</p><p>Continue to level one, and take \\(s\\) to be the maximum resulting rank cutoff. Increasing a cutoff strengthens high-rank exclusion. Proposition 1 then supplies a fixed source power \\(t\\) with value below \\(1/(2s^2)\\). Dimension- and range-independent inverse and list bounds ensure that changing \\(t\\) changes none of the already fixed agreement constants.</p><p>Now fix an accuracy smaller than one tenth of every tolerance needed for:</p><p class=\"li\">original pair-success transfer, reserving loss below \\(\\varepsilon^2/4\\);</p><p class=\"li\">upper defined-equality transfer, reserving loss below \\(c^2/16\\);</p><p class=\"li\">Lemma 5.4’s transfer, with loss at most \\(\\alpha q_j/16\\);</p><p class=\"li\">lower full-law transfer, with loss at most \\(\\gamma_j^2/64\\).</p><p>Reserve separately posterior discrepancy below \\(\\gamma_j^2/64\\), and require each relevant clean-game bound below \\(\\gamma_j/4\\). The factor ten accommodates the triangles of at most three raw comparisons.</p><p>Choose \\(n_p\\) upward. At that stage, the relevant finite maximum likelihood bound \\(L_p\\) and positive clean bound \\(p_{0,p}\\) depend only on already chosen lower branching and fixed parameters. As \\(n_p\\) tends through cubes to infinity,\n\\[\nL_p/\\sqrt{n_p}\\to0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(1+n_p^{-4/3}L_p^2)^{n_p}-1}\\to0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{2n_p^{-2/3}L_p}{1-n_p^{-2/3}}\\to0,\n\\qquad\n(1-n_p^{-2/3}p_{0,p}/64)^{n_p}\\to0.\n\\]\nThus all finitely many requirements can be met. Higher branching enlarges exterior records and assembly maps, but not raw call counts or child laws, so earlier bounds persist.</p><p>Proposition 3 gives completeness and at-most-two fibers. Suppose an unsatisfiable input had a labeling of value at least \\(\\varepsilon\\). Proposition 5 gives some \\(i&lt;j\\) with decoder agreement at least \\(\\gamma_j\\). Proposition 6 gives normalized rank-at-most-\\(s\\) agreement at least \\(\\gamma_j/2\\). Proposition 7, using the selected source power and branching, bounds the same event below \\(\\gamma_j/4\\), a contradiction.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Completing every fiber to size two — paper Section 8.3.** Let a preliminary weighted game have nonempty legal answer sets, all projection fibers of size at most two, perfect completeness, and NO value less than …", "statement": "<p>**Completing every fiber to size two — paper Section 8.3.**</p><p>Let a preliminary weighted game have nonempty legal answer sets, all projection fibers of size at most two, perfect completeness, and NO value less than \\(\\varepsilon=\\delta/3\\). Suppose the legal answer sizes are bounded by fixed \\(L_{\\max},R_{\\max}\\).</p><p>Choose an integer \\(q\\) with\n\\[\nq\\ge\\max\\{2,R_{\\max},\\lceil1/\\delta\\rceil\\},\n\\qquad\n2q-L_{\\max}\\ge6/\\delta.\n\\]\nEmbed legal left answers in \\([2q]\\) and right answers in \\([q]\\), using fixed orders.</p><p>Replacing each edge by all exact-two completions, with uniform conditional weights, gives a weighted exact \\(2\\)-to-\\(1\\) game with the same vertices and common alphabets. Perfect completeness is preserved, and its NO value is at most\n\\[\n\\varepsilon+\\delta/3.\n\\]\nFor fixed parameters the number of completions per edge is a constant.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a legal edge map \\(\\pi:L\\to R\\), the residual capacity at \\(b\\in[q]\\) is\n\\[\nc_b=2-|\\pi^{-1}(b)|\\in\\{0,1,2\\},\n\\]\ntaking zero legal preimages outside \\(R\\). Capacities sum to\n\\[\nN=2q-|L|.\n\\]\nEnumerate all maps from the \\(N\\) illegal left labels into these capacities. Every completion is an exact-two map.</p><p>For fixed illegal left label \\(a\\) and right label \\(b\\), symmetry among illegal labels gives\n\\[\n\\Pr_{\\widehat\\pi}[\\widehat\\pi(a)=b]\n=c_b/N\\le2/N\\le\\delta/3.\n\\]\nThis is pointwise in the answers chosen by any labeling.</p><p>A legal-left/illegal-right pair never accepts. Extend illegal choices of an enlarged-alphabet labeling to arbitrary legal answers while retaining legal choices. Every edge it satisfied with two legal answers remains satisfied by the legal extension, so such accepting weight is at most preliminary value. All remaining acceptance, arising from illegal left answers, is at most \\(\\delta/3\\). This proves soundness.</p><p>All completions agree with original maps on legal answers. A completeness labeling therefore satisfies every completed occurrence, not merely their average.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Explicit enumeration and removal of weights — paper Section 8.4.** Use the fixed parameters of Proposition 8 and the exact-two completion of Proposition 9.", "statement": "<p>**Explicit enumeration and removal of weights — paper Section 8.4.**</p><p>Use the fixed parameters of Proposition 8 and the exact-two completion of Proposition 9.</p><p>The completed weighted game can be enumerated deterministically in polynomial time as a polynomial list of positive-weight entries\n\\[\ne_1,\\ldots,e_m\n\\]\nwith explicit projection tables and rational weights \\(w_1,\\ldots,w_m\\) summing to one, each of polynomial binary bit complexity.</p><p>It can then be replaced, in deterministic polynomial time, by a nonempty explicit unweighted multiset of exact-two edge occurrences whose value differs, for every labeling, by at most \\(\\delta/3\\). Perfect completeness is preserved.</p>", "proof": "<p>The source power, tree leaf count, row counts, and local answer-domain sizes are constants. If the PCP instance has size \\(N_{\\mathrm{PCP}}\\), all ordered outer question tuples can be enumerated in\n\\[\nN_{\\mathrm{PCP}}^C\n\\]\ntime for a fixed \\(C\\). Repeated IDs stay separate coordinates.</p><p>For each tuple construct local spaces from finite truth tables. Pointwise multiplication, linear algebra, support reductions, joint partitions, and canonical keys can be computed by exhaustive enumeration on constant-size domains. The recursive sampler likewise has a finite enumeration of constant-size random choices. Question IDs need polynomially many bits. Identify equal keys and explicitly list all vertices and maps.</p><p>Uniform finite-space choices have constant denominators. Source-question probabilities have polynomial bit complexity, and completions add only constant denominators. Thus the positive-weight list has polynomial size and polynomial-bit rational weights.</p><p>Set\n\\[\nK=\\lceil3m/\\delta\\rceil,\\qquad k_a=\\lfloor Kw_a\\rfloor.\n\\]\nAdd one to each of the first \\(K-\\sum_ak_a\\) entries. The remainder is an integer in \\([0,m)\\), so each entry receives at most one extra copy and\n\\[\n\\sum_ak_a=K.\n\\]\nAll copies lie in original positive support. Moreover,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{TV}((w_a)_{a=1}^m,(k_a/K)_{a=1}^m)\n\\le m/K\\le\\delta/3.\n\\]</p><p>Output \\(k_a\\) copies of \\(e_a\\), each with its explicit exact-two table. Since\n\\[\nK&lt;3m/\\delta+1=O(m)\n\\]\nfor fixed \\(\\delta\\), rational arithmetic and writing all copies are polynomial-time. The list is nonempty, and every labeling’s acceptance changes by at most TV. Retaining only original support preserves completeness.</p><p>For syntactically trivial cases certified during preprocessing, fixed games with the same alphabets suffice. A single exact-two edge is satisfiable. For a fixed unsatisfiable output, take one vertex on each side and list all exact-two maps uniformly as parallel occurrences. Every fixed answer pair accepts a \\(1/q\\le\\delta\\) fraction by symmetry.</p><p>All constants may be hardcoded for the fixed rational \\(\\delta\\); no efficient dependence on \\(\\delta\\) is required.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 2, "cite"], [3, 0, "cite"], [3, 11, "cite"], [3, 34, "cite"], [3, 35, "cite"], [3, 38, "cite"], [4, 3, "cite"], [5, 3, "cite"], [6, 0, "cite"], [6, 28, "cite"], [6, 31, "cite"], [6, 38, "cite"], [7, 8, "cite"], [8, 10, "cite"], [9, 10, "cite"], [10, 6, "cite"], [11, 0, "cite"], [11, 19, "cite"], [11, 25, "cite"], [11, 27, "cite"], [11, 38, "cite"], [12, 11, "cite"], [12, 17, "cite"], [13, 11, "cite"], [13, 12, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [13, 17, "cite"], [14, 11, "cite"], [15, 11, "cite"], [15, 14, "cite"], [15, 16, "cite"], [16, 11, "cite"], [17, 11, "cite"], [18, 11, "cite"], [18, 17, "cite"], [19, 0, "cite"], [19, 25, "cite"], [19, 26, "cite"], [19, 27, "cite"], [19, 31, "cite"], [19, 32, "cite"], [19, 38, "cite"], [20, 19, "cite"], [21, 19, "cite"], [21, 22, "cite"], [22, 19, "cite"], [23, 19, "cite"], [24, 19, "cite"], [25, 0, "cite"], [25, 31, "cite"], [25, 34, "cite"], [25, 37, "cite"], [25, 38, "cite"], [26, 25, "cite"], [26, 27, "cite"], [27, 25, "cite"], [27, 28, "cite"], [27, 29, "cite"], [28, 25, "cite"], [28, 29, "cite"], [28, 30, "cite"], [29, 25, "cite"], [29, 38, "cite"], [30, 25, "cite"], [31, 0, "cite"], [31, 38, "cite"], [32, 31, "cite"], [33, 31, "cite"], [34, 0, "cite"], [34, 38, "cite"], [35, 34, "cite"], [35, 37, "cite"], [36, 34, "cite"], [36, 37, "cite"], [37, 34, "cite"], [38, 0, "cite"], [38, 40, "cite"], [39, 0, "cite"], [39, 40, "cite"], [40, 0, "cite"]]});
